CET6.passages({"basic":{"1":{"w":["a","able","abortion","about","above","abroad","accountant","across","adaptation","addicted","adjustment","admirable","adolescence","afraid","after","afternoon","again","age","agenda","ago"],"title":"A Year Abroad","t":"Ten years **ago**, Lin, then a quiet **accountant** at a small firm, decided to spend **a** year working **abroad**. Her friends were **afraid** she would regret it. Having grown up in a tiny town, she had never travelled **across** a border before, and the **adjustment** proved far harder than she had expected. **After** a long week at the office, she often spent the **afternoon** of her day off walking along the river, thinking **about** home.\n\nYet the experience taught her a great deal about **adaptation**. She learned to cook with unfamiliar ingredients, to keep a careful **agenda** of meetings in a foreign language, and to ask for help **again** and again without feeling embarrassed. Her colleagues found her determination **admirable**. One of them, a former debate champion, once took her to a public forum where people argued calmly over difficult issues such as **abortion** and gun control, something she had rarely witnessed at home.\n\nLooking back, Lin believes that no **age** is too late for change. Her teenage son, now deep in **adolescence** and **addicted** to video games, sometimes asks her why she left. She tells him that living abroad made her **able** to see her own life from **above**, as if from a mountain top, and that this view was worth every lonely night."},"2":{"w":["agree","agreement","agricultural","ahead","air","airmail","airport","airspace","alcoholic","all","allergic","alley","almost","along","already","also","always","amazing","american","among"],"title":"Letters by Airmail","t":"My grandfather **always** said that the most precious news arrived by **airmail**. In the 1980s he worked for an **American** company that sold **agricultural** machinery to farmers in remote villages, and his letters described the fields, the weather and the people he met **along** the way. My grandmother kept **all** of them in a box under her bed.\n\nBefore his first trip, the two of them had reached an **agreement**: he would come home every spring, no matter how busy he was. She did not **agree** easily, because she knew how long the journey could be. There was no **airport** within three hundred kilometres, and in those years even the **airspace** over the border region was closely controlled.\n\nHis letters were full of small, **amazing** details: a lantern festival in a narrow **alley**, a farmer who was **allergic** to the very wheat he grew, a village where **alcoholic** drinks had been banned because of a quarrel decades earlier. **Among** the stories, my favourite is about a storm that forced him to sleep in a barn for three nights.\n\nWhen I read them now, I can **almost** smell the fresh mountain **air**. He has **already** passed away, but his words are **also** a reminder that the future is never simply waiting **ahead** of us; it grows out of the promises we keep."},"3":{"w":["and","anecdote","angry","animal","announcement","annoy","another","answer","any","anybody","anything","apple","april","apron","arm","armchair","around","arrive","as","asian"],"title":"The Shelter on the Edge of Town","t":"Every Saturday in **April**, my aunt volunteers at an **animal** shelter on the edge of town. She ties on a faded **apron**, rolls up her sleeves **and** spends the morning feeding dogs that seem to have been forgotten by **anybody** who once loved them.\n\nLast spring the shelter made an **announcement** that worried everyone: it would close unless it found **another** source of funding. My aunt was **angry** at first. \"If people can spend money on **anything** they like,\" she said, \"why can't they spare a little for creatures that depend on us?\" But anger, she soon realised, was not an **answer**.\n\nSo she started collecting stories. Her favourite **anecdote** concerns an elderly **Asian** gentleman who would **arrive** every afternoon with a bag of sliced **apple** for a shy brown dog. He would sit in a worn **armchair** in the corner, the dog resting its head on his **arm**, **as** if the two of them had known each other for years. Visitors who heard this story rarely left without making a donation.\n\nSome neighbours complained that the barking began to **annoy** them, yet even they came **around** in the end. Today the shelter is still open, and my aunt insists that **any** problem becomes smaller once people are willing to listen to each other."},"4":{"w":["ask","assistance","astronomer","at","athletics","attentively","august","aunt","autonomous","autumn","away","awesome","baby","back","backache","bad","bag","bakery","ball","ballpoint"],"title":"Watching the Sky","t":"Last **August**, my **aunt**, an amateur **astronomer**, invited me to spend a week with her in a small village far **away** from the city lights. Each night we climbed the hill behind her house **at** around ten o'clock, carrying a telescope, a flask of tea and a **bag** of bread from the local **bakery**.\n\nI had always thought of stargazing as a lazy hobby, but it turned out to be surprisingly demanding. After an hour of bending over the telescope I had a terrible **backache**, and I was too tired to **ask** questions. My aunt, on the other hand, watched the sky **attentively**, recording each observation with an old **ballpoint** pen. \"Patience matters more than equipment,\" she told me. \"Even **bad** weather can teach you something.\"\n\nShe explained that she belonged to an **autonomous** research network in which amateurs share their data with universities. Professional scientists often rely on their **assistance** to track comets and variable stars. Before retiring, she had coached **athletics** at a secondary school, and she still approached every task with the energy of a coach on a training field.\n\nOn the last night, a meteor shot across the sky like a burning **ball**. \"That was **awesome**!\" I shouted, as excited as a **baby** seeing snow for the first time. When I went **back** to the city that **autumn**, I bought a telescope of my own."},"5":{"w":["banana","bank","barbecue","barbershop","basket","basketball","bathrobe","bathtub","battleground","be","beancurd","bear","beat","beautiful","because","become","bed","bedclothes","bedroom","beef"],"title":"House Rules","t":"When my cousin rented his first apartment after graduation, he quickly learned that a small home can easily **become** a **battleground**. His roommate wanted to hang a **basketball** hoop on the **bedroom** door; he wanted quiet. One insisted on cooking **beef** every night, while the other preferred **beancurd** and vegetables. Even the **bathtub** caused arguments, **because** neither of them liked cleaning it.\n\nThings changed one Sunday morning. My cousin came home from the **barbershop** with a fresh haircut and found his roommate standing in the kitchen in a **bathrobe**, holding a **basket** full of fruit. \"I think we should talk,\" the roommate said, peeling a **banana**. They sat on the floor beside a pile of freshly washed **bedclothes** and drew up a list of rules.\n\nThe list was simple: no music after eleven, separate shelves in the fridge, and a **barbecue** on the balcony every Friday to which both of them could invite friends. They also agreed to put a little money each month into a shared **bank** account for repairs.\n\nThey had expected their old habits to **beat** the new rules within a week, but the opposite happened. Within a month the apartment felt calm, even **beautiful**. My cousin admits he can still hardly **bear** the sound of a bouncing ball, but he now sleeps soundly in his own **bed**. Sharing a home, he says, is less about space than about learning to **be** patient."},"6":{"w":["beehive","beer","before","begin","behind","believe","bell","belly","below","bent","beside","better","between","big","bike","bingo","biochemistry","biography","bird","birdcage"],"title":"The Beekeeper's Notebook","t":"My neighbour, Mr. Zhou, taught **biochemistry** for thirty years **before** he retired to a cottage at the foot of a hill. Most mornings he rides an old **bike** down the lane, a basket of tools tied to the back and a straw hat pulled low over his eyes. **Behind** the cottage stands his pride and joy: a white wooden **beehive**, humming with life.\n\nHe has always had a **bent** for patient observation. \"People **believe** bees work at random,\" he told me, \"but every one of them knows its job.\" He showed me how the colony keeps its temperature **between** thirty-four and thirty-six degrees, and how a single careless move can turn a calm hive into a **big** problem. When the queen is about to leave with a swarm, he rings a small brass **bell**, an old custom his grandfather swore by.\n\nNot everything around the cottage is about bees. **Beside** the door hangs an empty **birdcage**; he set the **bird** free years ago, he says, because it sang **better** outside. On summer evenings he sits on the porch with a glass of cold **beer**, patting his round **belly** and laughing at his own jokes. On Fridays he walks to the village hall to play **bingo** with old friends.\n\nNow he has decided to **begin** a new project: a **biography** of his grandfather, the man who first taught him to listen to the hum **below** the surface of things."},"7":{"w":["birthday","birthplace","bit","black","blackboard","block","blow","blue","boat","boating","body","book","bookcase","bookmark","bookshelf","bookshop","bookstore","boom","bored","boring"],"title":"The Little Bookshop by the Lake","t":"For my twentieth **birthday**, I took a train to my grandmother's **birthplace**, a small town beside a lake in the south. I expected the trip to be **boring**; the guidebook described the place in a single dull paragraph. Instead, I found a town in the middle of an unexpected tourism **boom**.\n\nIts main street, barely one **block** long, was lined with cafés and shops painted **blue** and white. At the far end stood a tiny **bookshop** run by a retired teacher. An old **blackboard** outside listed the day's recommendations in neat chalk, and inside, every wall was covered by a **bookshelf** that reached the ceiling. \"A big chain has opened a **bookstore** in the next city,\" the owner said, \"but people still come here to talk.\"\n\nShe showed me a **book** of local history and a hand-painted **bookmark** her students had made decades ago. When I admitted that I had often been **bored** by history lessons at school, she laughed. \"Then you were reading the wrong books.\" From a glass **bookcase** she pulled out a photo album bound in **black** leather, and there was my grandmother at sixteen, **boating** on the lake.\n\nThat afternoon I rented a small **boat** myself. A cold wind began to **blow** across the water, and my whole **body** shook, but I stayed out a **bit** longer than I had planned, trying to see the town the way she once had."},"8":{"w":["borrow","botanical","botany","both","bottle","bowling","box","boxing","boy","bravery","bread","break","breakfast","breakthrough","breathless","brewery","bride","bridge","bright","bring"],"title":"A Wedding in the Old Brewery","t":"Last May my cousin, a **boxing** coach, married a **bride** who had spent six years studying **botany**. They had met, of all places, at a **bowling** alley, where she was trying in vain to **break** her own record and he offered, rather boldly, to give her a few tips. \"It took real **bravery**,\" she joked in her wedding speech, \"because he was terrible at bowling.\"\n\nThe ceremony was held in a former **brewery** that had been turned into a restaurant, right next to the city's **botanical** garden. Guests crossed a wooden **bridge** over a stream to reach the hall, and each of them received a small **box** of flower seeds and a glass **bottle** of homemade lemonade. The couple had asked everyone to **bring** a book instead of a gift, so that they could start a little library at the school where the bride now teaches.\n\nThe night before, nobody slept much. My sister and I **both** stayed up decorating the hall, and by morning the bride's younger brother, a lively **boy** of ten, was **breathless** from running errands. **Breakfast** was nothing more than coffee and **bread**, yet it felt like a feast.\n\nFor the bride, the day was a personal **breakthrough**: after years of shyness, she spoke in front of two hundred people without notes. Several guests had to **borrow** tissues from me. The future, she said, looked as **bright** as the spring sunshine pouring through the old brewery windows."},"9":{"w":["british","brochure","brother","brotherhood","brown","brunch","brush","buffet","build","building","bun","bungalow","bureaucratic","bus","businessman","businesswoman","busy","but","buy","by"],"title":"Two Brothers, One Business","t":"My uncle and his younger **brother** grew up in a **brown** brick **bungalow** on the edge of a fishing town. Their father sold steamed **buns|bun** from a cart near the **bus** station, and from an early age the two boys dreamed of owning a real shop. Today my uncle is a successful **businessman** who runs a chain of bakeries, **but** he still remembers the smell of that cart.\n\nWhen they decided to **build** their first café, everything seemed to go wrong. The licensing process was so **bureaucratic** that they had to submit the same documents five times, and a **British** investor who had promised to **buy** a share pulled out at the last minute. For months they were too **busy** to see their families. What kept them going, they say, was a sense of **brotherhood** that no setback could weaken.\n\nThe café finally opened in an old **building** near the harbour. They printed a simple **brochure** with photos of the sea and offered a weekend **brunch** **buffet** that soon drew queues on Saturday mornings. Customers loved the hand-painted walls; my uncle had picked up a **brush** himself to finish them **by** opening day.\n\nTheir success also inspired my aunt, who has since become a **businesswoman** in her own right, selling the café's recipes online. Running a family business, she likes to say, is like kneading dough: it takes strength, patience and a little warmth."},"10":{"w":["bye","cab","call","can","caption","car","card","care","careful","carry","case","cat","catch","celebration","certainly","chair","chairwoman","challenging","change","changeable"],"title":"Rescue in the Rain","t":"The weather in our city is famously **changeable**, and on the evening of my sister's graduation **celebration** it **certainly** lived up to its reputation. Bright sunshine turned into heavy rain within minutes, and our plans for a picnic had to **change** at once.\n\nWe decided to **call** a **cab** instead of driving the **car**, because the roads were already flooding. Just as we were leaving, my sister noticed that her **cat** was missing. In any other **case** we would have waited until morning, but she was far too worried. \"How **can** I celebrate while he is out in this storm?\" she said, and refused to say **bye** to anyone until we promised to help.\n\nSearching in the dark proved more **challenging** than we expected. We had to be **careful** on the slippery steps, and I tried to **carry** a torch and an umbrella at the same time. At last we found him hiding under a garden **chair** in the neighbour's yard, soaked and furious. It took three of us to **catch** him.\n\nBack home, the **chairwoman** of the neighbourhood committee, who had joined the search, laughed when she saw us. She wrote a short **caption** under the photo she had taken, \"Graduates Rescue Tiger\", and printed it on a greeting **card** for my sister. It reminded us that the people who truly **care** about you are the ones who show up in the rain."},"11":{"w":["chant","chat","cheap","check","chef","chick","child","chinese","choir","chopsticks","cinema","city","class","classroom","clean","cleaner","clear","click","clone","close"],"title":"The Dumpling Class","t":"Every Thursday evening, a **Chinese** **chef** named Wei turns his small restaurant in the old part of the **city** into a **classroom**. The tables are pushed together, aprons are handed out, and a dozen students, from a retired **choir** singer to a curious ten-year-old **child**, gather around him to learn how to make dumplings.\n\nThe **class** is surprisingly **cheap**, because Wei is less interested in money than in company. While the students fold dough, he likes to **chat** about the history of each dish. He insists that everyone use **chopsticks** rather than forks, and he makes the rules **clear** from the first lesson: wash your hands, keep your station **clean**, and **check** the pot before you walk away.\n\nSome evenings are chaotic. Once a new student tried to **clone** Wei's famous sauce by following a recipe on his phone, **click** after click, only to burn it completely. Another time a fluffy yellow **chick**, the pet of a neighbour's son, wandered in through the back door, and the whole room began to **chant** its new name, \"Little Dumpling,\" until it fell asleep in a basket.\n\nAfter class, the students sometimes walk together to the **cinema** across the street, leaving the restaurant to the **cleaner**, an old friend of Wei's who refuses to be paid. \"Food brings strangers **close**,\" Wei says. \"That is the real recipe.\""},"12":{"w":["clothes","cloud","cloudy","cocoa","coffee","coke","college","come","comma","company","computer","conversation","cook","cooker","cookie","cool","copy","coral","cordless","corner"],"title":"A Café Called The Comma","t":"Near my **college** there is a tiny café called The **Comma**. Its owner, a former journalist, chose the name because she believes everyone needs a short pause in the middle of a busy sentence. The place sits on a quiet **corner**, and on **cloudy** afternoons it is full of students hiding from their deadlines.\n\nThe menu is short but generous. You can order strong **coffee**, hot **cocoa** with cinnamon or an ice-cold **coke**, and every drink **comes|come** with a homemade **cookie**. The owner likes to **cook** everything herself on an old electric **cooker** in the back, and she keeps a **copy** of every recipe in a notebook she refuses to put on a **computer**.\n\nWhat I love most is the **conversation**. Strangers share tables, and you never know what you will learn. Last week a marine biologist explained to me why **coral** reefs lose their colour when the sea grows too warm; the week before, an elderly tailor told me how fashions in **clothes** have changed since the 1970s. Even the owner joins in, carrying a **cordless** phone in case her suppliers call.\n\nWhen I leave, the evening air is usually **cool**, and a thin **cloud** often hangs over the hills. I walk home slowly, feeling that the best **company** is sometimes found by accident."},"13":{"w":["cost","could","country","countryside","courtyard","cover","cowboy","crayon","credit","crossing","crossroads","crowded","cry","cuisine","cup","cut","cyclist","dad","dance","dark"],"title":"A Long Ride Home","t":"When my **dad** turned fifty, he surprised everyone by deciding to ride a bicycle across the **country**. He had never been a serious **cyclist**, and the plan seemed likely to **cost** him far more than money. Yet he trained for a year, and one spring morning he set off from our **crowded** apartment block with a small tent and a battered **cup** tied to his backpack.\n\nHis messages from the road were short but vivid. He described a **crossing** of a wide river on an old ferry, a village **dance** that went on until midnight, and a farmer in a wide hat who looked like a **cowboy** from an old film. He sampled the local **cuisine** wherever he stopped, and more than once he slept in the **courtyard** of a friendly family who refused his money.\n\nHalfway through the journey he reached a **crossroads**, both literally and in his mind. Heavy rain had **cut** off the road ahead, and he wondered whether he **could** go on. He called my mother and admitted that he almost wanted to **cry**. She reminded him that he did not need to **cover** every kilometre; the point was to see the **countryside** he had always admired from train windows.\n\nHe came home three months later, thin and sunburnt. On our kitchen wall now hangs a map that my little niece decorated in **crayon**, tracing his route. Whenever my dad tells the story, he gives the **credit** to the strangers who helped him, especially on the **dark** nights when he felt most alone."},"14":{"w":["darkness","database","date","daughter","day","dead","deadline","dear","december","decide","decoration","deep","deeply","delighted","desk","develop","development","dialogue","dictionary","die"],"title":"The Night Before the Deadline","t":"It was a cold night in **December**, and the office was almost empty. Mrs. Liang sat at her **desk**, staring at a screen full of numbers. The **deadline** for her team's report was nine o'clock the next morning, and the **database** that held three years of customer records had suddenly stopped responding.\n\nShe thought of her **daughter**, who had a school concert that evening, and felt a **deep** pang of guilt. She had promised to be there. Now the **date** was circled in red on her calendar for a very different reason.\n\nAt eleven the power failed, and the room fell into total **darkness**. For a moment she wondered whether the whole project was **dead**. Then she found a torch in a drawer, beside an old **dictionary** and a box of Christmas **decoration** lights left over from last year. She strung the lights across the window, laughed at herself and kept working by their soft glow.\n\nBy three in the morning the system was back. She **decided|decide** not to chase every number; instead she would describe the **development** of the main trends in plain language and **develop** a short list of recommendations. When her manager read the report the next **day**, he was **delighted**. \"**Dear** me,\" he said, \"this is the clearest thing you have ever written.\"\n\nThat evening she sat down with her daughter for a long **dialogue** about the concert she had missed. She was **deeply** moved when the girl said, \"Mum, you always told me never to let a good idea **die**. I'm glad you didn't, either.\""},"15":{"w":["different","difficult","dig","dinner","dinosaur","dirty","disability","disabled","disappointment","discuss","disgusting","dish","disobey","disturbing","do","doctor","dog","doll","dollar","door"],"title":"Digging for a Dinosaur","t":"Last summer my nephew Leo, who was eight, decided to **dig** for a **dinosaur** in his grandparents' back garden. He had seen a documentary about fossils and was certain that a giant skeleton lay hidden under the vegetable beds. His grandmother, a retired **doctor**, did not have the heart to tell him otherwise.\n\nFor three days he worked with a small spade, his clothes growing more **dirty** by the hour. The family **dog** followed him everywhere, barking at every worm. His little sister offered to help, but only if her favourite **doll** could supervise from a chair by the kitchen **door**.\n\nOn the fourth day he found something: a long, curved bone. The whole family gathered round to **discuss** it over **dinner**. His grandfather examined it and announced, with great seriousness, that it was **different** from any dinosaur bone he had ever seen. In fact it was the bone of a cow, probably buried decades ago, and it smelled **disgusting**.\n\nLeo's **disappointment** was obvious, and he refused to touch a single **dish** that evening. His grandmother told him that being wrong is not **disturbing** at all; it is simply part of how science works. She also told him about a famous fossil hunter with a physical **disability** who made major discoveries despite being told that fieldwork was too **difficult** for **disabled** people.\n\nThe next morning Leo was back in the garden. He had been told not to dig near the roses, and for once he did not **disobey**. He even sold a few \"fossils\" to his relatives for a **dollar** each, promising to **do** better next time."},"16":{"w":["double","down","download","downtown","draw","dress","drier","drink","drive","driver","dry","dumpling","during","dustbin","dusty","dvd","dynasty","each","ear","early"],"title":"The Day Before Spring Festival","t":"**Early** on the morning before Spring Festival, my uncle, a taxi **driver**, picked me up at the train station. \"We have a lot to do,\" he said, and pulled into the traffic heading **downtown**. He loves to **drive**, but even he admitted that the holiday rush was enough to make anyone feel **down**.\n\nOur first stop was his mother's flat, where the whole family was preparing for the New Year's Eve dinner. **Each** of us had a job. My cousins folded **dumpling** after dumpling at the kitchen table, while my aunt tried to **dry** her freshly washed **dress** with a hair **drier**, because the rain had made it impossible to hang anything outside. Grandpa sat in the corner with a hot **drink**, telling anyone who would listen about the Tang **Dynasty**, his favourite period of history.\n\nI was sent to tidy the storeroom, a **dusty** space full of old furniture. I filled the **dustbin** twice with broken toys and discovered a box of my grandmother's **DVD** collection, mostly costume dramas from the 1990s. My little nephew asked me to **draw** a dragon on a red paper lantern, then begged me to **download** a cartoon for him on my phone.\n\n**During** dinner the noise was so loud that I could barely hear the person speaking right into my **ear**. Grandpa raised his glass and wished everyone **double** happiness in the coming year. Outside, fireworks began to light up the sky."},"17":{"w":["earth","east","eastwards","easy","eat","educator","egg","eggplant","eight","eighth","either","eleven","else","email","empty","encouragement","end","english","enjoy","enjoyable"],"title":"A Teacher Who Moved East","t":"Ms. Grant is a retired **educator** from Canada who has taught **English** in **eleven** countries. For most of her career she moved steadily **eastwards**, from Europe to the Middle East and finally to a small mountain town in western China. \"I always felt pulled toward the **east**,\" she says, \"as if the **earth** itself were turning me in that direction.\"\n\nHer life there was simple but **enjoyable**. She lived in a small house with an **empty** courtyard, rose at **eight** every morning and walked to school past fields of **eggplant** and chilli. Teaching was not always **easy**: some of her students had never met a foreigner, and at first they were too shy to speak at all. What changed everything, she believes, was **encouragement**. She praised every attempt, however small, and soon even the quietest pupils raised their hands.\n\nShe also learned a great deal from them. They taught her to **eat** with chopsticks, to fry an **egg** with tomato the local way, and to sing folk songs she still remembers. When she could not find something at the market, a student always knew someone **else** who sold it.\n\nIt is now the **eighth** year since she retired, but she keeps in touch with former students by **email**, and many of them have become teachers themselves. Nothing, she says, makes her **enjoy** life more than hearing about their classes. Asked whether she misses the travelling or the teaching more, she laughs and says she cannot choose **either**. \"The **end** of teaching,\" she adds, \"is not knowledge but courage.\""},"18":{"w":["enough","eraser","european","even","evening","ever","every","everybody","everyone","everything","everywhere","exam","example","except","excuse","exercise","expect","expensive","explorer","eye"],"title":"Exam Week","t":"In the week before the final **exam**, our dormitory turned into a strange little world. **Everybody** studied **everywhere**: on the stairs, in the laundry room, even in the corridor at midnight. My roommate kept an **eraser** behind her ear like a carpenter's pencil and filled notebook after notebook with formulas.\n\nI tried to follow her **example**, but by Wednesday **evening** I felt that I had learned nothing. **Everything** I read seemed to slip away the moment I closed the book. I began to invent an **excuse** for **every** break: a walk, a snack, a phone call home. My mother listened patiently and then asked, \"Have you **ever** tried taking a real rest?\"\n\nSo I did. I went for a run along the river, my first proper **exercise** in days. On the way I passed a bookshop window displaying the diary of a famous **European** **explorer** who had crossed Antarctica. The book was far too **expensive** for a student, but I stood there reading the first page through the glass. He wrote that on the hardest days he did not **expect** to reach the end; he only tried to walk far **enough** to see the next hill.\n\nThat line had caught my **eye**, and it stayed with me. **Except** for one difficult chemistry question, the exam went better than I had feared. When **everyone** gathered afterwards to compare answers, I realised that **even** the strongest students had been just as nervous as I was."},"19":{"w":["eyewitness","face","facial","factory","fail","fairness","fall","family","famous","far","farm","fast","fat","father","february","feel","few","field","fifteen","fifth"],"title":"The Night the Roof Fell","t":"One cold night in **February**, heavy snow caused part of the roof of an old **factory** on the edge of our town to **fall** in. Luckily, the night shift had ended **fifteen** minutes earlier, and no one was hurt. The only **eyewitness** was a **farm** worker driving home along the road beside the **field**.\n\nThe next morning a reporter interviewed him. He was a large man with a **fat** woollen scarf around his neck and a **face** red from the wind, and his **facial** expression changed several times as he spoke, from shock to relief. \"I heard a sound like thunder,\" he said. \"At first I thought I was imagining things, but I could **feel** the ground shake even from **far** away.\"\n\nThe factory had been the town's most **famous** employer for decades. My **father** worked there for twenty years, and so did half the families on our street. For many people, the accident showed how badly the place had aged. A **few** workers complained that the managers had wanted to cut costs **fast** and had ignored warnings about the old building.\n\nIn the weeks that followed, the town held meetings about **fairness** in compensating the workers who had lost shifts. The company promised not to **fail** them, and the mayor visited the site for the **fifth** time that winter. For my **family**, it was the first time we talked seriously about what the town might become without the factory."},"20":{"w":["fifty","fill","film","find","fine","fingernail","finish","fire","firefighter","firewood","firmly","first","fish","fit","five","flaming","flashlight","flight","floor","flower"],"title":"Twenty-Five Years in Uniform","t":"My neighbour Chen has been a **firefighter** for more than half of his **fifty** years, and he is still as **fit** as a man of thirty. When I asked him to describe his **first** real emergency, he smiled and told me about a winter night in a mountain village.\n\nA family had been drying **firewood** beside an old stove, and a spark had set the pile alight. By the time his team arrived, **flaming** pieces of wood were dropping from the roof onto the **floor** below. The power was out, so they worked by **flashlight**, moving slowly from room to room. It took them **five** hours to **finish** the job.\n\n\"People think the hardest part is the **fire**,\" he said, \"but it's actually the waiting afterwards.\" He described standing in the snow while the family tried to **find** their belongings. An old woman held **firmly** onto a pot of winter **flowers|flower**, the only thing she had carried out. A boy kept asking whether his pet **fish** had survived; luckily, the tank was untouched.\n\nChen now trains young recruits and shows them a short safety **film** before every exercise. He also visits schools, where children **fill** the hall with questions. One asked why he keeps every **fingernail** cut so short; another wanted to know whether he had ever taken a **flight** in a rescue helicopter. He answers them all patiently. The best reward, he says, is hearing a family tell him that they are **fine**."},"21":{"w":["fluency","fly","foggy","food","foot","football","for","foreign","forget","forgetful","form","format","forty","forward","founding","four","fourteen","fourth","franc","free"],"title":"French at Forty","t":"At **forty**, my aunt decided to learn French. She had always loved **foreign** films, and after the **founding** of a small language school in our neighbourhood, she finally had no excuse. \"If I don't start now,\" she said, \"I'll **forget** the idea for another ten years.\"\n\nClasses were held **four** evenings a week. There were **fourteen** students, ranging from a teenage **football** player to a retired pilot who used to **fly** to Paris every month. The teacher used an unusual **format**: no textbooks for the first month, only songs, games and **food**. Each student had to bring a dish and describe it in French.\n\nMy aunt struggled at first. She is a little **forgetful**, and she often walked to class on **foot** through the **foggy** streets, repeating new words under her breath, only to lose them the moment she arrived. She even filled in the enrolment **form** for the next level twice by mistake.\n\nBy the **fourth** month, however, she noticed real progress. Her **fluency** was still limited, but she could hold a conversation **for** several minutes without panicking. One evening the teacher showed the class an old **franc** coin and explained how French money had changed before the euro; my aunt kept it as a lucky charm.\n\nNow she is looking **forward** to a trip to Lyon next spring. Learning a language in middle age, she says, has made her feel **free** in a way she had not felt since university."},"22":{"w":["freeway","freezing","friday","fried","friend","friendly","friendship","from","front","fruit","full","fun","furnished","future","gale","game","garden","gardening","gate","get"],"title":"Weekends at the Cabin","t":"Every **Friday** in winter, my oldest **friend** and I drive out of the city on the **freeway** to her family's cabin in the hills. The cabin is small but fully **furnished**, with a wood stove, two narrow beds and a shelf **full** of board games.\n\nLast weekend the weather was **freezing**. A **gale** blew all night, rattling the windows and bending the trees in the **garden**. We stayed inside, playing a card **game** we had invented as children and eating **fried** rice with whatever vegetables and **fruit** we could find in the fridge. It was simple, but it was great **fun**.\n\nThe cabin belongs to her grandfather, who still drives up every spring to do the **gardening**. He is a **friendly** man who greets every visitor at the **front** door as if they were family. He once told us that a lasting **friendship** is like a garden: it needs time, patience and a little attention every season.\n\nOn Sunday morning the wind finally dropped. We walked down to the old wooden **gate** at the end of the lane and watched the sun rise over the fields. My friend asked me what I expected **from** the **future**. I said I did not know, but I hoped we would still **get** to spend weekends like this when we were old."},"23":{"w":["gift","gifted","giraffe","girl","give","glad","glass","glasshouse","go","goldfish","good","goodbye","grade","graduation","gram","grandchild","grandma","grandpa","grandparent","granny"],"title":"Grandpa's Glasshouse","t":"When I was a little **girl**, my **grandpa** kept a **glasshouse** at the bottom of his garden. Inside it grew tomatoes, orchids and a lemon tree that produced exactly one lemon a year. On sunny afternoons the **glass** walls turned the place into a warm, green world, and I would sit there for hours watching the **goldfish** in a small stone pond.\n\nMy **grandma**, whom everyone in the village called **Granny** Li, was the practical one. She weighed every **gram** of flour when she baked and kept careful notes on which seeds did well. Grandpa, by contrast, was a **gifted** storyteller. He told me that the tallest sunflower in the glasshouse was really a **giraffe** in disguise, and for years I half believed him.\n\nEvery **grandparent**, I think, wants to **give** something that lasts. For my **graduation** from primary school, he gave me a **gift** I still treasure: a notebook in which he had drawn every plant he had ever grown. \"You were always **good** at noticing things,\" he wrote on the first page. \"Keep your eyes open, whatever **grade** you get.\"\n\nYears later, when I had a child of my own, I took her to see the old glasshouse before it was sold. She was the youngest **grandchild** in the family, and she ran from plant to plant shouting with delight. As we said **goodbye** to the place, I felt sad, but I was also **glad**. Some things have to **go** so that their stories can travel further."},"24":{"w":["grass","great","green","greengrocer","grill","ground","group","grow","gruel","guess","guest","gym","gymnastics","ha","hair","half","hand","handbag","handtruck","happily"],"title":"The Greengrocer on Our Street","t":"Mr. Ho has been the **greengrocer** on our street for almost thirty years. Every morning at six he wheels crates of vegetables out of his van on a battered **handtruck**, stacking cabbages, carrots and **green** beans in neat rows on the pavement. He knows almost everyone by name and remembers what each **guest** in his little shop likes to buy.\n\nHe grew up in the countryside, where his parents taught him how to **grow** vegetables on a small plot of land. \"In those days we often had nothing but rice **gruel** for breakfast,\" he told me, \"but the food tasted **great** because we had grown it with our own **hands|hand**.\"\n\nOn summer evenings he fires up a small **grill** outside the shop and invites neighbours to taste roasted corn. A **group** of children from the local **gym** often stops by after **gymnastics** practice, their **hair** still wet, and sits on the **grass** of the little park across the road. One evening a girl asked him to **guess** how old she was. He studied her seriously and said \"twenty-five\", and she burst out laughing: \"**Ha**! I'm nine!\"\n\nLast month a woman accidentally left her **handbag** on the counter. Mr. Ho closed the shop for **half** an hour and walked around the neighbourhood until he found her. She thanked him **happily**, and he simply shrugged. For him, the shop is not just a business; it is the common **ground** on which the whole street meets."},"25":{"w":["happy","hard","hardworking","harmless","hat","have","he","head","headmistress","headteacher","health","healthy","hear","hearing","heart","heavy","hello","help","her","here"],"title":"The New Headmistress","t":"When Mrs. Wu became **headmistress** of our village school, many parents were doubtful. The previous **headteacher** had been a strict man who rarely smiled, and **he** had run the school the same way for twenty years. Mrs. Wu was different. On her first morning she stood at the gate in a bright red **hat**, saying **hello** to every child by name.\n\nShe believed that a **healthy** body and a **happy** mind go together. She replaced sugary snacks in the canteen with fruit, started a morning running club and invited a nurse to talk to the pupils about **health** and sleep. She also arranged free **hearing** tests, after which three children who had been labelled lazy turned out simply to have trouble following the teacher.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. Some teachers complained that her ideas meant more **heavy** work for them, and one or two parents thought her plans were a waste of money. Mrs. Wu listened to every complaint. \"I **hear** you,\" she would say, \"and I want to **help**.\" She was **hardworking** herself, often staying until late in the evening, and slowly people began to trust **her**.\n\nThe changes were mostly small and **harmless**, yet together they transformed the school. Five years later, when she announced her retirement, the hall was full. A former pupil, now a doctor, stood at the **head** of the stage and said that Mrs. Wu had taught them that learning does not **have** to be **hard** to matter. \"She led with her **heart**,\" he said, \"and that is why we are all **here** today.\""},"26":{"w":["hers","herself","hey","hi","hibernate","hibernation","high","hill","hilly","him","himself","his","hive","hobby","hold","holiday","home","homeland","hometown","homework"],"title":"Winter in the Hills","t":"My cousin Mei grew up in a **hilly** region in the north, and every winter **holiday** she goes back to her **hometown** to help her parents on their small farm. Last year she invited my brother and me to come with her.\n\nOn the first evening, her father took us up the **hill** behind the house to show us the bee **hive** he had wrapped in straw for the cold months. Bees do not really **hibernate**, he explained, but they cluster together to stay warm, which looks a little like **hibernation** from the outside. My brother, who has made insects his **hobby**, was delighted, and he asked so many questions that Mei's father finally laughed and told **him** to come back in spring.\n\nMei was busier than any of us. She cooked, chopped wood and still found time for her university **homework** in the evenings. \"This place is my **homeland**,\" she said, \"even though my life is in the city now.\" Her old bedroom was full of school prizes; one shelf was **hers**, the other her sister's.\n\nOn our last day we climbed to the **highest|high** point of the valley. \"**Hey**!\" my brother shouted to a shepherd far below, and the man waved back, calling \"**Hi**!\" in return. Mei stood quietly, trying to **hold** the view in her memory. My brother, true to **himself**, was already planning **his** next trip. I realised that for Mei, going **home** was not about the past at all; it was a way of reminding **herself** who she was."},"27":{"w":["hooray","hope","horse","hospital","hot","hotdog","hotel","hour","house","housework","how","hundred","hungry","hunter","hurry","husband","ice","idea","if","ill"],"title":"A Week on the Grassland","t":"Last summer my sister and her **husband** booked a week at a small **hotel** on a **horse** farm in Inner Mongolia. It was their first real holiday in years; until then their lives had been an endless cycle of office work and **housework**.\n\nThe owner, a former **hunter** who now protects the wildlife he once chased, met them at the gate. \"Here you never need to **hurry**,\" he said. Breakfast was served at whatever **hour** guests got up, and the old wooden **house** behind the stables had no television, only shelves of books.\n\nOn the third day the weather turned very **hot**. My sister felt **ill** after a long ride and spent the afternoon resting, and the owner's wife brought her a bowl of **ice** and some cold tea. **If** she had not felt better by evening, they would have driven to the nearest **hospital**, a **hundred** kilometres away. Luckily, by sunset she was **hungry** again and happily ate a **hotdog** grilled over the campfire, the owner's one concession to \"foreign food\".\n\nThat night, sitting under a sky full of stars, her husband had an **idea**: why not come back every year? \"**Hooray**!\" my sister cried, laughing at her own excitement. When she told me about the trip, she said she still did not understand **how** such a simple place could feel so restful. \"I **hope** we never lose that feeling,\" she added."},"28":{"w":["important","in","information","instead","interesting","into","irrigate","it","its","itself","january","jeep","jewelry","job","join","juice","juicy","july","jump","june"],"title":"Water at the Edge of the Desert","t":"**In** **January**, an engineer named Hassan took a **job** with a project that helps farmers **irrigate** land at the edge of a desert. On his first morning he climbed into an old **jeep** and drove three hours across rough tracks to reach the village. It was a long way from the city, but he found the work more **interesting** than anything he had done before.\n\nThe village had a well, but **its** water was too salty for most crops. **Instead** of drilling deeper, Hassan's team installed a simple drip system that delivered water straight **into** the roots of each plant. Farmers were invited to **join** training sessions, where they shared **information** about soil and weather. The most **important** lesson, Hassan says, was that the system had to be easy to repair; a machine that only an expert can fix is soon abandoned.\n\nBy **June** the first melons were ripening. In **July** the village held a small festival, and children ran from stall to stall drinking fresh **juice** and eating **juicy** slices of watermelon. One woman proudly showed him a piece of silver **jewelry** she had bought with the money from her first harvest.\n\nPrices for fruit at the regional market began to **jump**, and the village suddenly had a future. Hassan still visits whenever he can. \"The desert does not change **itself**,\" he likes to say, \"but **it** can be persuaded to cooperate under the right conditions.\""},"29":{"w":["just","kangaroo","keep","key","kilo","kind","kindergarten","kitchen","knife","knock","know","labourer","lake","land","language","large","last","late","latest","laugh"],"title":"A Kangaroo in the Playground","t":"My friend Sophie moved to a small town in Australia **last** year to teach at a **kindergarten** beside a **lake**. Before she left, she did not **know** a single word of the local slang, and she worried that **language** would be her biggest problem.\n\nOn her first day, a boy came running into the classroom shouting that a **kangaroo** was standing in the playground. Sophie thought he was joking, but when she looked out of the window, there it was, chewing grass as calmly as a cow. The children were not surprised at all; for them it was **just** another morning. The head teacher simply asked everyone to **keep** quiet and let the animal hop away.\n\nThe school runs on a tight budget. The cook, a former farm **labourer**, prepares lunch for forty children in a tiny **kitchen** with one sharp **knife** and an enormous pot. He buys rice by the **kilo** and vegetables from farmers who own **land** around the lake, and parents often **knock** on the kitchen door to drop off fresh eggs or bread.\n\nSophie soon found that the **key** to her new life was patience and a sense of humour. When she arrived **late** for a staff meeting after getting lost, everyone burst out **laughing|laugh**, and she laughed with them. In her **latest** email, she wrote that she has never met such **kind** people, and that a **large** part of her heart now belongs to that little town."},"30":{"w":["league","learn","leave","left","leftover","leg","lemonade","lend","lesson","let","letter","library","lie","life","light","like","listen","little","live","long"],"title":"The Library Volunteer","t":"Every Saturday my grandfather volunteers at the public **library** in our town. He is seventy-six and walks with a slight limp in his **left** **leg**, an old injury from his years in a local football **league**, but he refuses to **let** it slow him down.\n\nHis job is to help older people **learn** to use computers. Many of them have spent their whole **life** without a keyboard, and some are afraid to touch one at all. My grandfather is patient. He tells them to **listen** carefully, take notes and never be ashamed to ask the same question twice. \"Every mistake is a **lesson**,\" he says.\n\nHe also runs a reading corner for children. He likes to **lend** them books from his own collection and asks each child to write him a short **letter** about the story. His favourite reply came from a girl of seven, who wrote: \"I **like** this book because it has a **little** dog in it and the dog never tells a **lie**.\"\n\nWhen the library closes, he does not **leave** straight away. He switches off every **light** himself, collects the **leftover** biscuits from the staff room and carries them home in a paper bag. Sometimes he stops at a café on the way for a glass of **lemonade**. He says he wants to **live** a **long** time, if only to find out what the children will become."},"31":{"w":["look","lot","loudly","love","lovely","low","lunch","machine","madame","mail","mailbox","make","man","manager","many","map","march","market","match","math"],"title":"Madame Rose's Café","t":"In a quiet street near the old **market** there is a tiny café run by an elderly Frenchwoman whom everyone calls **Madame** Rose. She came to our city forty years ago, fell in **love** with a local **man** and never left. Her café serves only one dish at **lunch**, chosen each morning according to whatever **looks|look** freshest at the market.\n\nBefore she opened the café, Madame Rose worked as a **manager** in a textile factory, where she taught herself **math** by doing the accounts at night. She still keeps her books by hand and refuses to use a computer or even a coffee **machine**. \"A **lot** of people think old methods are slow,\" she says, \"but they help me **make** fewer mistakes.\"\n\nThe walls are covered with a large **map** of France and postcards sent by former customers from **many** countries. Every few days she walks to the **mailbox** at the corner to collect her **mail**, then reads each card **loudly** to whoever happens to be sitting nearby. In **March**, when her birthday comes around, the postcards arrive by the dozen.\n\nSome days business is **low**, and she admits that she sometimes feels tired. But then a regular customer arrives with flowers, or two strangers at the same table strike up a conversation and discover that they are a perfect **match** for a game of chess. On such days, she says, life is simply **lovely**."},"32":{"w":["matter","may","maybe","me","meal","mean","meaning","meat","medicine","meet","meeting","member","memorize","mend","mentally","mention","menu","messy","middle","milk"],"title":"Grandmother Stays Home","t":"When my grandmother turned eighty last **May**, the family held a **meeting** to decide whether she should move into a care home. I was the youngest **member** of the family at the table, and I remember feeling that nobody had asked her what she wanted.\n\nSo the next weekend I visited her alone. Her kitchen was as **messy** as ever, with jars of herbs on every shelf and a basket of socks waiting to be **mended|mend**. She put a glass of warm **milk** in front of **me** and began to cook, as she always did, without looking at a **menu** or a recipe book. \"People think old age is about forgetting,\" she said, chopping **meat** for dumplings. \"It isn't. I can still **memorize** a phone number faster than you.\"\n\nShe was right, of course. Physically she was slowing down, but **mentally** she was as sharp as ever. She told me that the doctors wanted her to take more **medicine**, and that she did not object, but that she would not give up the simple pleasure of cooking a **meal** for her family. \"If you **mean** to help me,\" she said, \"help me stay here.\"\n\nI asked her what home really meant to her. Its **meaning**, she explained, lay not in the house itself but in the daily routine: going to the market, chatting with neighbours, sitting in the **middle** of the courtyard on summer evenings. \"**Maybe** that sounds foolish,\" she added, \"but it is what keeps me going.\"\n\nWhen the family met again, I made sure to **mention** everything she had told me. It did not **matter** that I was the youngest; they listened. In the end we arranged for a nurse to visit twice a week, and my grandmother still lives in her own home. Whenever I **meet** her now, she reminds me that I am always welcome for dinner."},"33":{"w":["million","mind","mine","mineral","minibus","miniskirt","minute","miss","mistake","mistaken","mm","modem","modern","mom","moment","monday","money","month","moon","more"],"title":"The Mining Town","t":"When I was ten, my **mom** took a job as an engineer with a **mineral** company, and we moved to a small town built around an old copper **mine**. On our first **Monday** there, she drove me to school in the company **minibus**, pointing out the huge machines and the hills of grey rock along the way.\n\nAt first I hated it. The town felt a **million** miles from the city, and the internet depended on an ancient **modem** that made strange noises every time it connected. I often thought I had made a terrible **mistake** in agreeing to come. My mom told me I was **mistaken**. \"Give it a **month**,\" she said. \"You might change your **mind**.\"\n\nShe was right. Within weeks I had friends, and I discovered that the town was more **modern** than I had imagined. There was a new library, a swimming pool and a music teacher in a bright **miniskirt** who taught us to play the guitar. My mother, meanwhile, worked long hours, measuring rock samples to the nearest **mm** and saving every spare piece of **money** for my education.\n\nI still remember the **moment** I knew I belonged there. One clear night, a friend and I climbed onto the roof of her house to watch the full **moon** rise over the mine. \"I'd **miss** this if I ever left,\" she said. I did not reply, but I felt exactly the same. A single **minute** like that, I learned, can be worth **more** than any city."},"34":{"w":["morning","most","mother","motherland","motivation","motorbike","motorcycle","motto","mountain","moustache","mouth","move","much","music","must","my","myself","name","nationwide","near"],"title":"My Father's Songs","t":"Every **morning** for thirty years, my father rode an old **motorbike** to the factory where he worked. He was a quiet man with a thick **moustache**, and he rarely said **much** at breakfast. His **motto**, written on a card above his desk, was simple: \"Do what you **must**, then do what you love.\"\n\nWhat he loved **most** was **music**. When he retired, he surprised us all by announcing that he wanted to travel across the **motherland** on a **motorcycle** and record folk songs in every province. My **mother** thought he was joking. \"At sixty-five?\" she said, her **mouth** falling open. But his **motivation** was stronger than her doubts.\n\nHe spent two years on the road. He crossed **mountain** passes in the west, slept in village guesthouses and recorded hundreds of songs, many of which had never been written down. When local radio stations heard about his project, they launched a **nationwide** campaign asking listeners to send him the **name** of any traditional singer living **near** them.\n\nToday his recordings are kept in a university archive, and students study them every year. I sometimes listen to them **myself** late at night. Each song reminds me that it is never too late to **move** in a new direction, and that **my** father's quiet life was richer than any of us knew."},"35":{"w":["nearly","necessary","necktie","need","neither","never","new","news","newspaper","next","nice","night","nine","ninety","ninth","no","nobody","noise","noisily","none"],"title":"Tomorrow's Paper","t":"Mr. Lu is **ninety** years old, and for **nearly** seventy of those years he has read a **newspaper** every morning. He still wears a **necktie** to breakfast, even when **nobody** else is at home, because he believes that reading the **news** is a serious business.\n\nHis granddaughter once tried to convince him that a printed paper was no longer **necessary**. \"You can read everything on your phone,\" she said. \"It's **new** every minute.\" Mr. Lu smiled politely and said **no**. He does not dislike technology, he explained; he simply prefers the **noise** of turning pages to the silence of a screen.\n\nThese days his eyes are weaker, so he reads with a magnifying glass. He **never** skips the local section, and he cuts out articles he finds interesting and pastes them into thick notebooks. He has filled **nine** of them in the past year alone. **Neither** his children nor his grandchildren have the patience to read them all, but **none** of them would dream of throwing one away.\n\nLast week, on the **night** before his birthday, the family threw a surprise party. The children ran **noisily** around the living room while the adults sang. It was the **ninth** surprise party they had held for him, and he still pretended to be astonished. When his granddaughter asked what he **needed|need**, he thought for a long moment. \"A **nice** cup of tea,\" he said, \"and tomorrow's paper. Whatever happens **next**, I want to read about it.\""},"36":{"w":["noodle","noon","nor","north","nose","not","note","nothing","novelist","november","now","numb","number","nurse","nursing","october","of","off","office","offshore"],"title":"The Night Nurse","t":"Every year, from **October** to **November**, cold winds blow down from the **north** and the small hospital in our coastal town gets busier. My aunt has worked there as a **nurse** for twenty years. She starts her shift at **noon** and often does **not** get home until long after midnight.\n\nMany of her patients are fishermen who work on **offshore** boats. They arrive with hands so cold that they are completely **numb**, or with injuries they have ignored for days. My aunt treats them with patience, but she is also strict. \"**Nothing** is more dangerous than pretending you are fine,\" she tells them, tapping a finger on the end of their **nose**.\n\nIn the **office** behind the ward, she keeps a notebook in which she writes a short **note** about every patient she has cared for. The **number** of entries is now well over ten thousand. A local **novelist** once asked to read it, hoping to find material for a book about **nursing**, but my aunt refused; the stories, she said, belong to the patients.\n\nWhen she finally takes her coat **off** at the end of a long night, she usually eats a bowl of hot **noodle** soup at the stall across the road. She has neither the time **nor** the energy to cook. Yet when I ask whether she would like to retire **now**, she laughs. \"Not yet,\" she says. \"So many **of** them still need someone to look after them.\""},"37":{"w":["often","oh","oilfield","old","omelette","on","once","one","only","open","opener","or","orange","order","other","ouch","our","ours","ourselves","out"],"title":"Omelettes in the Desert","t":"My uncle **once** spent ten years as a cook on an **oilfield** in the desert. When I was a child, he **often** told me stories about that time, and the one I remember best is about an **omelette**.\n\nThe camp was a long way from anywhere, and supplies arrived **only** every two weeks, so he had to be creative. **One** winter the delivery truck broke down, and the workers had eaten everything except eggs, a sack of onions and a crate of **orange** juice. My uncle decided to cook the best omelettes they had ever tasted.\n\nHe stood beside the **old** stove for hours, keeping it alight with whatever scraps of wood he could find. At one point he burned his hand and cried \"**Ouch**!\" so loudly that half the camp came running. The workers laughed, then rolled up their sleeves and helped. Someone found a rusty tin **opener**, someone else fetched water, and slowly the kitchen filled with the smell of frying onions.\n\n\"**Oh**, those omelettes were nothing special,\" my uncle says now, \"but they put everyone in a good mood.\" That night, he told me, the men kept the canteen **open** late, played cards **or** sang songs, and for a few hours the hardship seemed to disappear. Nobody gave anyone an **order**; everyone simply looked after each **other**.\n\nWhen I asked him what he learned **out** there, he thought for a moment. \"That **our** problems feel smaller when we share them,\" he said. \"The food was **ours**, but the laughter belonged to everyone. We kept **ourselves** going **on** very little.\""},"38":{"w":["outgoing","outing","outside","outspoken","over","overweight","own","paddle","page","pair","palace","pancake","paper","paperwork","pardon","parent","park","parking","parrot","party"],"title":"A Day at the Summer Palace","t":"Last spring my parents organised a family **outing** to an old summer **palace** outside the city. My father is an **outgoing** man who loves crowds, while my mother is more **outspoken** and has strong opinions about everything, so the planning took longer than the trip itself.\n\nWe set off early in my father's **own** car and spent twenty minutes looking for a **parking** space near the gate. Inside, the **park** was beautiful: lakes, willow trees and long covered walkways painted with scenes from history. My younger brother, who is a little **overweight** and hates walking, complained until we rented a small boat and he was allowed to **paddle** it **over** the lake himself.\n\nAt lunchtime we sat **outside** a café and ate **pancake** rolls filled with roast duck. A **parrot** in a cage by the door kept repeating \"Hello! Hello!\" until my mother finally said, \"**Pardon**?\" and everyone burst out laughing. My brother bought a **pair** of cheap sunglasses and refused to take them off for the rest of the day.\n\nEvery **parent**, I think, wants their children to remember days like this. My mother, who spends most of the week buried in **paperwork** at the tax office, carried a small notebook and wrote a few lines on a new **page** whenever we stopped. Later she printed some photos on glossy **paper** and glued them inside. She calls it the best **party** she never had to plan."},"39":{"w":["pass","passage","passport","past","pay","pen","pence","pencil","people","per","perfect","performer","person","personally","photo","photographer","pianist","piano","pick","picture"],"title":"The Lost Passport","t":"The young **pianist** stood at the airport counter, searching every pocket of her coat. Her **passport** had vanished. In three hours she was supposed to **pass** through security and board a flight to Vienna, where she would play the **piano** in her first international competition.\n\nA **photographer** travelling with her team tried to help. He went through his own bags, then checked every **photo** he had taken that morning, hoping to spot where she might have left it. In one **picture**, taken at a café, the passport was clearly visible on the table beside a **pen** and a **pencil**.\n\nThey rushed back. The café owner had kept it safely behind the counter and refused to accept any money. \"You don't need to **pay** me,\" he said. \"Just play a **perfect** concert.\" He even gave her a handful of British coins, a few **pence** left behind by a tourist, \"for luck\".\n\nAfter the competition, in which she came second, she **personally** thanked him in a long letter. She wrote that she would never forget the kindness of a stranger who expected nothing in return. As a **performer**, she said, she had always thought of the audience as a crowd; now she saw each **person** in it as someone with a story of their own.\n\nIn the **past** few years she has played in many countries, and she often tells this story to young musicians. **People** remember kindness, she says, far longer than any performance. Before each concert she reads a short **passage** from her diary about that day. Once a year she also gives free concerts for travellers at the airport, two hours **per** day for a week, and lets the audience **pick** the programme."},"40":{"w":["piece","pioneer","pity","place","plan","plane","plant","plate","play","playmate","playroom","please","pleased","pleasure","policeman","polite","poor","popcorn","pork","possible"],"title":"The Playroom","t":"When my nephew was six, he had a **playmate** named Tom who lived across the street. Tom's family was **poor**, and his parents worked long hours at a factory that processed **pork**. Tom often came over after school, and my sister turned the spare bedroom into a **playroom** where both boys could build forts out of cardboard and blankets.\n\nMy sister made one rule: everyone had to be **polite**. You said \"**please**\" when you asked for a toy and \"thank you\" when you got it. The boys took the rule seriously. Once, when Tom accidentally broke a **plate** while carrying **popcorn** to the playroom, he apologised so earnestly that she could not help laughing. \"What a **pity**,\" she said, \"but it's only a **piece** of china.\"\n\nThe boys loved to **play** at being explorers. One week they **planned|plan** an expedition to the North Pole; the next, they built a cardboard **plane** and flew it to the moon. Tom said he wanted to be a **pioneer** like the astronauts he had read about. My nephew wanted to be a **policeman**, mostly because of the uniform.\n\nYears later, Tom became an engineer at a solar power **plant**. When he visited us last summer, he told my sister that the playroom had been the happiest **place** of his childhood. She was deeply **pleased**. \"Having you here was a real **pleasure**,\" she said, \"and I always knew you would do everything **possible** and a little more.\""},"41":{"w":["post","postbox","postcard","postcode","potato","practice","present","preview","price","printing","problem","pub","publicly","pull","punctuate","punctuation","pupil","push","put","puzzled"],"title":"Sixty Years of Postcards","t":"My grandfather has collected postcards for more than sixty years. His first **postcard**, sent by a cousin from Shanghai in 1962, still sits in a wooden frame above his desk, and his collection now fills twelve albums. Each time a new one arrives, he reads the message twice, checks the **postcode** and the stamp, and slips it carefully into a plastic sleeve.\n\nWhen I was a **pupil** at primary school, he gave me an unusual **present** for my birthday: a stack of blank cards and a promise. \"If you send me one every month,\" he said, \"I will answer every single one.\" I was **puzzled** at first, but it soon became a game. Every few weeks I walked to the red **postbox** at the corner and dropped in a card about school, my friends or a **problem** I could not solve.\n\nHis replies were always worth waiting for. He wrote in neat handwriting and liked to **punctuate** his sentences with tiny drawings. He taught me that **punctuation** matters as much as spelling, and he would gently **push** me to correct my mistakes. Once he even **put** a little **potato** stamp, carved by hand, on a card just to make me laugh.\n\nToday he rarely goes to the **post** office himself, so I go for him. Sometimes we meet at the little **pub** near his flat, where he reads my cards aloud, almost **publicly**, to the regulars. Stamps, he complains, now cost twice the **price** they used to, and **printing** has become so cheap that people forget the value of a handwritten line. But **practice**, he insists, keeps the habit alive. Last week he showed me a **preview** of his newest album and asked me to **pull** out my favourite card. I chose the very first one I ever sent him."},"42":{"w":["pyramid","quake","quarter","question","quick","quiet","quite","radio","railway","rain","raincoat","rainfall","raise","read","ready","really","reason","rebuild","receipt","receive"],"title":"After the Quake","t":"When a strong **quake** shook the mountain town where my cousin teaches, the **railway** line was cut and the only news came from an old **radio** in the school office. My cousin, who had been preparing a lesson about the builders of the Egyptian **pyramid**, suddenly found herself running a shelter for two hundred people.\n\nThe first night was the hardest. Heavy **rain** fell for hours, and the weather service reported the highest **rainfall** in a decade. Families huddled under plastic sheets, and my cousin gave her own **raincoat** to an elderly man who was shivering. Nobody slept; the only sounds were the rain on the roof and the **quiet** voices of parents comforting their children.\n\nHelp arrived **quickly|quick** once the roads reopened. Within a **quarter** of an hour the next afternoon, trucks of food and water appeared, and the volunteers were **ready** to work around the clock. Each family signed a **receipt** when they **received|receive** supplies, so that nothing went missing.\n\nThe children kept asking the same **question**: would the school be able to **rebuild** in time for the new term? My cousin did not know, but she found a **reason** to stay hopeful. Every evening she gathered the pupils in a circle to **read** stories aloud, and slowly their fear began to fade. \"We cannot stop earthquakes,\" she told them, \"but we can **raise** our voices together and help each other.\"\n\nMonths later, the school did reopen. My cousin admits that she was **quite** frightened during those first days, but she says that was when she **really** learned what a community means."},"43":{"w":["receptionist","red","redirect","regards","remember","report","rest","restaurant","return","reuse","reviewer","rewind","rewrite","rhyme","rice","rich","ride","right","ring","river"],"title":"The Restaurant by the River","t":"Last autumn a food **reviewer** from a national magazine visited a small **restaurant** by the **river** in our town. The owner, Mrs. Fang, had no idea who he was. The young **receptionist** greeted him with a smile, showed him to a table by the window and brought him a cup of roasted **rice** tea.\n\nThe menu was short and written by hand on a board painted **red**. Mrs. Fang believes in simple cooking: fresh fish from the river, vegetables from local farms and sauces that are **rich** but never heavy. She also tries to **reuse** everything she can, from glass jars to leftover bread, and she proudly told her guest that her kitchen produces almost no waste.\n\nThe reviewer later wrote a glowing **report**. He described the meal as \"a poem with every **rhyme** in the **right** place\" and promised to **return** as soon as he could. When the article appeared, the phone began to **ring** all day. So many people called that Mrs. Fang had to **redirect** calls to her daughter's mobile phone.\n\nShe told me that she had to **rewrite** her whole business plan to cope with the crowds, and some evenings she wishes she could **rewind** time to the quiet days before the review. But she will always **remember** the reviewer's kindness. Every year he sends a card with warm **regards**, and every summer he takes a long bicycle **ride** along the river and stops at her restaurant to **rest**."},"44":{"w":["road","room","round","rugby","run","running","sad","sailing","salesgirl","saleswoman","salty","same","saturday","say","saying","school","schoolbag","schoolmate","science","sea"],"title":"Saturdays by the Sea","t":"Every **Saturday** morning, my old **schoolmate** Jake and I take the coastal **road** to a small fishing village by the **sea**. We have done the **same** thing for nearly ten years, ever since we left **school**, and the trip has become a kind of ritual.\n\nJake teaches **science** at a secondary school, but at weekends he forgets about exams and spends hours **sailing** a little boat he built himself. I prefer **running** along the beach while he is out on the water. When we were younger, we both played **rugby** for the school team; now our knees remind us that those days are over.\n\nOur first stop is always the same shop. The **salesgirl**, a cheerful teenager, sells us coffee and **salty** fish cakes, while her mother, an experienced **saleswoman**, tries to persuade us to buy souvenirs we do not need. \"As the **saying** goes,\" she tells us every week, \"a day by the sea is never wasted.\"\n\nLast month, after Jake's father passed away, I worried that the trip would feel **sad** without its usual laughter. We drove in silence, and Jake did not **say** much. But at the harbour he pulled an old **schoolbag** out of the car. Inside were photos of his father teaching him to sail. We sat in the **room** above the café, looked at every picture and passed them **round** the table. Then Jake stood up and said, \"Come on, let's **run** to the lighthouse, just like he used to.\""},"45":{"w":["seagull","seashell","season","seat","seaweed","second","secondhand","secretary","see","seem","sell","semicircle","send","september","serve","service","set","settler","seven","seventh"],"title":"The Bookshop by the Harbour","t":"In **September**, when the tourist **season** ends, the seaside town where my aunt lives becomes quiet again. She runs a small **secondhand** bookshop near the harbour, and autumn is her favourite time of year. \"This is when the real readers come,\" she says.\n\nThe building is more than a century old; it was put up by a **settler** from the south who arrived with nothing but a boat and a box of books. Its front window is shaped like a **semicircle**, and on windy days you can **see** waves breaking against the rocks below. A **seagull** often lands on the windowsill, as if it too were curious about the books.\n\nMy aunt does not just **sell** books. She offers a free reading **service** to old people who can no longer see well, and twice a week she will **serve** them tea while she reads aloud. Her best friend, a retired **secretary** from the town hall, helps her keep the accounts and **send** books by post to customers in other cities.\n\nThe shop is full of small treasures: a basket of **seashell** necklaces made by local children, a jar of dried **seaweed** snacks and a comfortable **seat** in the corner where you can read for hours. Every **seventh** customer receives a free bookmark, a tradition she **set** up in her very first week.\n\nBusiness does not always **seem** good; some months she sells only a few dozen books. But on the **second** floor, where she lives, hangs a photo of the day she reopened the shop, **seven** years ago. Looking at it, she says, reminds her why she started."},"46":{"w":["several","shall","sharpener","shaver","she","sheep","shine","ship","shirt","shoe","shooting","shop","short","shorts","should","shout","show","sick","side","sideway"],"title":"The Film Crew in the Village","t":"Last summer a film crew arrived in my grandparents' village for the **shooting** of a historical drama. For **several** weeks the quiet streets were full of cameras, lights and actors in costume. The director, a tall woman with a loud voice, would **shout** \"Action!\" so often that even the village children began to copy her.\n\nMy grandmother, who has kept **sheep** for forty years, was asked to appear in one scene. She had to lead her flock along a narrow **sideway** behind the temple while the leading actor rode past on a horse. She was so nervous that she felt **sick** the night before. \"What **shall** I wear?\" she kept asking. In the end the crew gave her an old cotton **shirt** and a pair of straw sandals instead of her usual rubber **shoes|shoe**.\n\nThe village **shop** did more business that month than in the whole previous year. The owner even started selling pencil **sharpener** sets and electric **shaver** kits to crew members who had forgotten their own. My cousin worked as a runner, carrying lunch boxes from one **side** of the set to the other in **shorts** and a cap.\n\nWhen the film was finally released, the whole village gathered to watch it. My grandmother's scene was very **short**, barely ten seconds, but **she** was delighted. \"You **should** have seen her face,\" my grandfather told me later. The sun seemed to **shine** a little brighter that day. She says that next time a film crew comes, she wants a bigger part, perhaps on a **ship**, and that the film will **show** the world how beautiful our village is."},"47":{"w":["silk","similar","simple","simply","since","sincerely","sing","single","sir","sister","sit","six","sixteen","sixteenth","sixth","size","skateboard","skillfully","skirt","sky"],"title":"The Silk Skirt","t":"My **sister** turned **sixteen** last spring, and for her **sixteenth** birthday our grandmother made her a **skirt** of blue **silk**. Grandmother has been sewing **since** she was a girl, and she works so **skillfully** that her stitches are almost invisible.\n\nThe design was **simple**: no lace, no buttons, just one piece of fabric that moved like water. Grandmother spent **six** evenings on it, measuring my sister's **size** again and again, because she believes a skirt that does not fit is worse than no skirt at all. When my sister tried it on, she said it was the colour of the summer **sky**.\n\nMy sister is not usually interested in clothes. She would rather spend her weekends on a **skateboard** in the park with her friends. But she wore the skirt to her birthday dinner and did not take it off all evening. At one point she stood up and began to **sing** an old folk song that Grandmother had taught her, while the rest of us tried to **sit** still and not cry.\n\nAt the end of the night, my sister thanked our grandmother **sincerely**. \"I don't need anything else,\" she said. A neighbour bowed to her like a knight in an old film and said, \"At your service, **sir**,\" then realised his mistake, and everyone laughed.\n\nIt was the **sixth** skirt Grandmother had made for a grandchild, and each one has been **similar** in shape but different in colour. She says she will make one for every **single** granddaughter, because it is **simply** the best way she knows to say \"I love you\"."},"48":{"w":["sleep","slow","small","smell","smelly","smile","smoke","smoker","smoking","sneaker","snow","snowball","snowman","so","socket","softball","soldier","some","somebody","someone"],"title":"Grandfather Quits","t":"For thirty years my grandfather was a heavy **smoker**. He started **smoking** as a young **soldier**, when cigarettes were handed out to keep the men awake during long night watches, and he never stopped. The **smell** of tobacco filled every corner of his **small** flat, and his old jackets were so **smelly** that my grandmother refused to wash them with anything else.\n\nThen one winter he caught a bad cough that would not go away. The doctor told him plainly that if he did not stop, **someone** else would have to look after him within a few years. He went home in silence, put his last packet in a drawer and never opened it again.\n\nThe first weeks were hard. He could not **sleep**, and he became irritable over the smallest things. **Somebody** suggested he take up a hobby to keep his hands busy, so he joined a **softball** team for retired people. He was **slow** at first, but he enjoyed it **so** much that he soon forgot about cigarettes for hours at a time. He even bought a pair of bright white **sneakers|sneaker**.\n\nThat winter, when the first heavy **snow** fell, he went outside with my little cousins and built a **snowman** taller than any of them. They threw a **snowball** at him, and he laughed so loudly that the neighbours looked out of their windows. Watching from the doorway, my grandmother could not help **smiling|smile**. \"The only **smoke** in this house now,\" she said, \"comes from the kitchen.\"\n\nThese days he plugs his radio into the old **socket** in the hallway and listens to it on his morning walks. He tells **some** of his old friends that quitting was the hardest battle he ever fought, and the best."},"49":{"w":["something","sometimes","somewhere","son","song","soon","sorry","sound","south","spare","speak","spend","spoken","spoonful","sport","spring","squid","stairs","stamp","stand"],"title":"Letters from the South","t":"My neighbour Mrs. Tan has a **son** who moved to the **south** of the country for work several years ago. He rarely has time to visit, but every **spring** he sends her a letter by post, with a colourful **stamp** in the corner and a small gift inside.\n\nMrs. Tan keeps the letters in a box at the top of the **stairs**. **Sometimes**, when I help her carry her shopping, she invites me in and reads one aloud. Her son writes about his job at a seafood company, about the **squid** and fish that arrive at the harbour every morning, and about the **sport** he plays with colleagues after work. He always ends with the same line: \"I'm **sorry** I can't be there.\"\n\nHis **spoken** Chinese, she says with a laugh, now carries a strong southern accent, but his written words have never changed. When he calls, they rarely **speak** for more than a few minutes; letters, she believes, let people say **something** they would never say aloud.\n\nOn evenings when she feels lonely, she makes a cup of tea, stirs in a **spoonful** of honey and listens to an old **song** on the radio. She does not like to **spend** money on herself, but she always keeps a **spare** room ready, just in case her son decides to come home **soon**. \"He is **somewhere** out there working hard,\" she said. \"That's enough for me.\" She cannot **stand** the idea of being a burden to him.\n\nLast month the letter contained a surprise: a train ticket and a note that read, \"Come and see the sea.\" When she showed it to me, the **sound** of her laughter filled the whole flat."},"50":{"w":["star","start","station","stay","still","stomachache","stop","stopwatch","store","story","straight","strange","street","strong","student","study","subject","subtraction","such","suffering"],"title":"A Conversation at the Station","t":"Late one winter evening, a university **student** named Kai missed the last bus and had to walk to the railway **station** along a dark **street**. He was carrying a heavy bag of books, and he had been **suffering** from a terrible **stomachache** all afternoon. All he wanted was to get home.\n\nAt the station, a **strange** thing happened. An old man sitting on a bench asked him the time, then looked at Kai's books and asked what **subject** he was taking. \"Maths,\" Kai said. The old man smiled and pulled a battered **stopwatch** from his pocket. \"I taught maths for forty years,\" he said. \"Do you know what my pupils found hardest? Not calculus, but **subtraction**. People hate to take things away.\"\n\nThey talked for an hour while they waited for the delayed train. The old man told Kai the **story** of his own student days, when he had to **study** by candlelight and walk ten kilometres to school. He spoke in a calm, **strong** voice, looking **straight** at Kai as if every word mattered, and Kai forgot all about his stomach.\n\nWhen the train finally arrived, the old man did not get on. He said he would **stay** a little longer to watch the **stars|star**. Kai asked whether he was **still** teaching. \"In a way,\" the old man laughed. \"Every conversation like this is a lesson.\"\n\nKai never saw him again, but he often thinks of that night. He has since decided to **start** a free tutoring club at his university, and he keeps a small **store** of chalk and notebooks for pupils who cannot afford them. Whenever he feels like giving up, he remembers that **such** kindness can **stop** a bad day from turning into a bad life."},"51":{"w":["sugar","suggest","suggestion","suite","summer","sun","sunburnt","sunday","sunglasses","sunny","superman","supermarket","supper","sure","surface","surprise","surrounding","swap","sweet","swim"],"title":"The Supermarket Swap","t":"Last **summer** my family rented a holiday **suite** in a small coastal town. The weather was **sunny** every day, and by the end of the first week my little brother was so **sunburnt** that he had to wear a T-shirt even when he went to **swim**.\n\nOn **Sunday** morning we walked to the local **supermarket** to buy food for a barbecue. At the checkout we met an elderly couple who were staying in the room next to ours. They had bought far too much **sugar** and not nearly enough bread, while we had the opposite problem. My mother laughed and made a **suggestion**: why not **swap**? The couple agreed at once, and by the time we left the shop we had become friends.\n\nThat evening they joined us for **supper** on the balcony. The old man told us that he used to work as a diver and had spent half his life beneath the **surface** of the sea. He described the **surrounding** reefs in such detail that my brother, who believes he is a kind of **superman**, wanted to start diving lessons the next day. \"Are you **sure**?\" my father asked. \"You can hardly stand the **sun**.\"\n\nOn our last day the couple gave us a **surprise**: a jar of **sweet** homemade jam and a pair of old **sunglasses** for my brother, \"for when you become a diver\". \"I **suggest** you keep them,\" the old man told him with a wink, \"because the brightest light is sometimes found underwater.\""},"52":{"w":["swimming","table","take","talk","tall","tasteless","tasty","taxi","tea","teach","teacher","teamwork","teapot","telephone","television","tell","ten","tennis","tenth","text"],"title":"The Tennis Teacher","t":"My aunt has been a **teacher** at a primary school for twenty years, but her real passion is **tennis**. Every weekend she coaches a group of children at the community courts, and she says she learns as much from them as they learn from her.\n\nShe believes that sport can **teach** lessons no textbook can. The children practise **teamwork** in doubles matches, and they learn to **take** defeat calmly. After each session they sit around a folding **table** in the clubhouse, drinking **tea** from a big old **teapot** and **talking|talk** about what went well.\n\nOne of her pupils, a **tall** girl of **ten**, was too shy to speak during the first few weeks. My aunt never pushed her. Instead she would **tell** short stories about her own mistakes as a young player, and slowly the girl began to open up. By the **tenth** lesson she was laughing with the others and asking for extra practice.\n\nMy aunt is old-fashioned in some ways. She refuses to **text** parents and prefers to **telephone** them instead, because she thinks a voice carries more warmth than a message. She rarely watches **television**, saying she would rather be outside. On rainy days, when the courts are closed, she takes the children **swimming** at the indoor pool, and afterwards they share snacks that range from **tasty** to completely **tasteless**, since she lets the children choose.\n\nLast spring the club won a regional junior trophy. When a reporter asked my aunt for her secret, she simply called a **taxi** to take the whole team out for noodles. \"That,\" she said, \"is the secret.\""},"53":{"w":["than","thank","thankful","that","the","their","theirs","them","themselves","then","there","thermos","these","they","thin","thing","think","thinking","third","thirsty"],"title":"Lost in the Mountains","t":"On the **third** day of their hiking trip, my parents and **their** friends found **themselves** lost in the mountains. **They** had left the main trail to look for a waterfall, and by the afternoon **the** path had disappeared completely. Everyone was tired and **thirsty**, and the only drink left was a **thermos** of lukewarm tea.\n\nMy father, who is usually calm, admitted later that he was frightened. \"I kept **thinking** about the children at home,\" he told me. But my mother insisted that panic would not help. \"Let's **think** clearly,\" she said. \"We came up this valley, so the river must be down **there**.\"\n\nThey followed the stream for two hours. The ground was steep, the air was **thin**, and **these** were hardly ideal conditions for people in their fifties. Yet nobody complained. When they finally reached a small farmhouse, an old farmer welcomed **them** inside. He gave them water and bread and refused to take any money. The **thing** my mother remembers most is that he said **that** helping strangers was simply what people did in the mountains. **Then** he drove them back to their car on his tractor.\n\nMy parents still talk about that day. They say the tea from the thermos tasted better **than** any drink they have ever had, and they will always be **thankful** to the farmer. Every New Year they send him a gift and **thank** him again, and he replies with a bag of walnuts. Whenever my mother cracks one open, she says the walnuts are a reminder that the best part of the journey was **theirs** to share."},"54":{"w":["thirteen","thirty","this","those","though","thousand","three","thriller","through","throw","thunderstorm","thursday","ticket","till","time","tired","to","today","together","tomorrow"],"title":"The Thursday Storm","t":"Last **Thursday** began like any other day. I had bought a cinema **ticket** for a new **thriller** that evening and planned to go straight from work. But at about **three** o'clock the sky turned black, and **those** of us near the office windows saw lightning flash across the city.\n\nThe **thunderstorm** lasted for nearly **thirty** minutes. Rain poured **through** the gaps in the old roof, and someone had to **throw** towels on the floor to stop people slipping. The building has **thirteen** floors, and the power went off on every one of them. Hundreds of computers shut down at the same **time**, and more than a **thousand** unsaved documents were lost.\n\n**Though** everyone was **tired** and frustrated, the mood soon changed. Without screens to stare at, people began talking to colleagues they had sat beside for years without really knowing them. Someone found candles; someone else made tea on a camping stove. We sat **together** in the dark and told stories **till** the lights came back.\n\nI never made it **to** the cinema, but I don't regret it. **Today** my colleagues and I still laugh about that afternoon, and we have agreed to switch off our computers for one hour **this** Friday, just to talk. Who knows? **Tomorrow** might bring another storm."},"55":{"w":["tonight","too","tooth","toothache","toothbrush","toothpaste","totally","tourism","tourist","tournament","town","train","trainer","translator","travel","traveler","tree","trip","trouble","true"],"title":"The Translator's Travels","t":"**Tonight** my cousin Ling is flying home after a three-month **trip** abroad. She works as a **translator** for a **tourism** company, and her job is to **travel** with groups of Chinese tourists, explaining menus, museum labels and local customs.\n\nIt sounds like a dream job, but she says it is not **totally** glamorous. Every **tourist** has a different problem. One man lost his **toothbrush** and **toothpaste** on the first night and refused to buy new ones, insisting that the hotel should provide them. Another woman broke a **tooth** on a hard bread roll and suffered from a terrible **toothache** for days before Ling found a dentist willing to see her.\n\nStill, she loves the work. She has seen famous cities and tiny villages, and she once watched a tennis **tournament** from the front row because a client had a spare ticket. In one small **town** in the mountains, she met an elderly **traveler** who had walked across three countries with nothing but a backpack. He told her that the best way to see a place is to take the slowest **train** you can find.\n\nHer biggest **trouble**, she admits, is homesickness. She keeps a photo of the old **tree** outside our grandmother's house in her wallet. \"It's **true**,\" she wrote in her last message. \"You can go anywhere in the world, but home is still the place you dream about.\" She joked that her fitness **trainer** will be shocked by how much she has eaten. When I said I envied her, she replied, \"Come with me next time. You'll be **too** busy to complain.\""},"56":{"w":["try","tuesday","turn","tv","twelfth","twelve","twentieth","twenty","twice","two","umbrella","unbelievable","uncle","unconditional","under","underground","understand","undivided","unhealthy","unimportant"],"title":"Smartphones on Tuesday Nights","t":"Every **Tuesday** evening, my **uncle** goes to a small community centre to **try** to teach older people how to use smartphones. He started the class **twelve** years ago, after his own father found modern technology completely **unbelievable**. \"He could **understand** a **TV**,\" my uncle says, \"but a phone with no buttons was a mystery.\"\n\nThe class meets in a room **under** the library, which can only be reached by an old staircase from the **underground** car park. On rainy nights my uncle stands at the entrance with a big black **umbrella**, waiting to walk each student down. His patience is **unconditional**; he will explain the same thing **twice**, three times, or as often as needed.\n\nSome of his students have **unhealthy** habits that he gently tries to change, such as staying up too late reading news on their phones. Others worry that their questions are **unimportant**. He tells them that no question is foolish, and he gives each person his **undivided** attention.\n\nLast month the centre held a party to celebrate the **twentieth** anniversary of the library. My uncle's class was the **twelfth** group to perform, and they surprised everyone with a video they had filmed and edited themselves. It lasted **twenty** minutes and showed **two** of the oldest students, both over eighty, video-calling their grandchildren abroad.\n\nWhen the video ended, the audience applauded for a long time. My uncle simply smiled. Then he **turned|turn** to his students and said, \"Same time next Tuesday?\""},"57":{"w":["unmarried","unrest","unsafe","unsuccessful","until","untrue","up","us","use","usually","vanilla","veal","vegetable","very","videophone","viewer","village","villager","violinist","visit"],"title":"The Violinist Comes Home","t":"When the young **violinist** Lin Xia decided to **visit** her home **village** after ten years abroad, few people recognised her. She had left as a shy, **unmarried** girl of eighteen, determined to study music in Vienna, and many neighbours had assumed she would never come back.\n\nHer first concert in Europe was **unsuccessful**, and for a while she wondered whether the gossip at home was **untrue**: perhaps she really did not have enough talent. But she kept practising **until** she was invited to join a respected orchestra. Her family **usually** watched her performances on a **videophone** her brother had set up, and every time she bowed, they cheered in front of the screen.\n\nWhen she arrived, the **villagers|villager** had prepared a feast: roasted **veal**, fresh **vegetable** dishes and a cake flavoured with **vanilla**, her favourite since childhood. In the evening she played in the courtyard of the old school, which had been declared **unsafe** years earlier and had just been repaired. More than two hundred people sat on benches and on the ground.\n\nThe region had been through some **unrest** in recent years, as young people left for the cities and old businesses closed. That night, however, the mood was **very** different. One elderly **viewer**, watching from a wheelchair, said the music reminded him of what the village could still be. \"You came back for **us**,\" he told her. She smiled and promised to **use** every chance to return. Then she picked **up** her violin and played one more song."},"58":{"w":["wait","wake","walk","walkman","wall","want","warm","wash","washroom","watch","water","watermelon","way","wayside","we","wear","weather","weatherman","website","wednesday"],"title":"A Wednesday with the Weatherman","t":"Mr. Zhao has been the **weatherman** on our local radio station for twenty-five years, and every **Wednesday** he records a special programme about the **weather** in the countryside. Last month he invited me to come along.\n\nWe had to **wake** up at five. He insisted that we **walk** the first part of the **way**, along a muddy **wayside** path between rice fields, because \"you cannot describe a place from a car window.\" He **wears|wear** the same faded jacket on every trip and carries an old **walkman**, which he uses to record the sounds of birds and running **water**.\n\nAt a small farmhouse, an old farmer invited us in. He showed us the **wall** where he marks the height of every flood, and his wife insisted that we **wash** our hands in the tiny **washroom** before eating slices of cold **watermelon**. \"**We** don't need a **website** to know when the rain is coming,\" the farmer laughed. \"We **watch** the sky.\"\n\nMr. Zhao listened carefully, taking notes. He told me later that this is why he loves his job: people **want** to be heard, not just informed. On the drive home he was quiet for a long time. Then he said that every forecast is really a promise to **wait** with people through the storm, and to tell them when the **warm** days will return."},"59":{"w":["week","weekend","weight","welcome","well","west","western","westerner","westwards","wet","what","when","where","whether","which","while","white","who","whom","whose"],"title":"Heading West","t":"Last **week** my friend Tom, a **westerner** who has lived in China for ten years, asked me a question I could not answer: \"**Which** part of the country do you know least?\" I realised that I had never travelled to the **west**. So that **weekend** we packed our bags and drove **westwards** for two days.\n\nThe landscape changed slowly. Green fields gave way to dry hills, and the air became thinner and cooler. **While** Tom drove, I read about the history of the **western** provinces, **where** the ancient Silk Road once carried merchants and monks across the deserts.\n\nOn the third day heavy rain fell, and we arrived at a small guesthouse completely **wet**. The owner, an old woman in a **white** headscarf, gave us towels and hot soup. She asked **who** we were and **whom** we had come to see. When Tom explained that we had come simply to look around, she laughed and said we were **welcome** to stay as long as we liked.\n\nThat evening she told us about her family. Her son works in a factory in the east, and she does not know **whether** he will ever come back. \"Young people go where the jobs are,\" she said. \"**What** can we do?\" She showed us a photo of a girl **whose** smile looked exactly like hers: her granddaughter, now studying medicine.\n\n**When** we left, she insisted on giving us a bag of dried fruit, even though it added extra **weight** to our already full car. Tom said it was the best trip he had made in China, and I am now **well** aware how little I knew about my own country."},"60":{"w":["why","wide","wife","wildlife","will","willingly","willingness","wind","windbreaker","window","winter","wish","with","without","woman","woo","word","work","workday","worker"],"title":"Waiting for Snow Leopards","t":"My neighbour's **wife**, Anna, is a **wildlife** photographer. She spends most of every **winter** in a hut on the edge of a nature reserve, waiting for snow leopards. When I asked her **why** she chose such a difficult job, she said simply, \"Because I **wish** to show people what we are about to lose.\"\n\nHer husband, a quiet engineer, supports her **willingly**, although he admits that he misses her. While she is away he sends her a short **word** of encouragement every evening, and on each **workday** he checks the mountain forecast before he leaves home.\n\nAnna told me that the hardest part is not the cold **wind** but the waiting. Sometimes she sits for days by a tiny **window** in her hide, **with** a camera on her knees and **without** seeing a single animal. \"You need endless patience and a real **willingness** to fail,\" she said. Local **workers|worker** at the reserve help her carry equipment up the narrow mountain paths, and she pays them fairly for their **work**.\n\nHer photographs have now been shown around the world, and conservation groups use them to **woo** donors and governments. A **wide** range of people, from schoolchildren to scientists, write to her after seeing them. The most touching letter came from an elderly **woman** who said she had never seen a leopard but now felt she knew one.\n\nAnna will return to the mountains next month in her old red **windbreaker**. \"I **will** keep going,\" she said, \"as long as there is something left to photograph.\""},"61":{"w":["workforce","workmate","workplace","works","world","worn","worried","worry","worth","would","wounded","write","wrong","year","yellow","yes","yesterday","yet","you","young"],"title":"The Old Works","t":"In the town where I grew up, everyone called the old steel factory \"the **works**\". For three generations it employed almost the entire local **workforce**. My grandfather started there at sixteen, and my father joined him when he was still **young**.\n\n**Yesterday** I visited the site for the first time in many **years|year**. The factory closed long ago, and the buildings are now an industrial museum. Walking through the empty halls, I saw my grandfather's **worn** gloves displayed in a glass case, next to a **yellow** safety helmet with his name on it.\n\nA guide explained that the **workplace** had been dangerous. Accidents were common, and in one terrible fire in 1978, eleven men were **wounded**. My father had told me about it only once. He said his **workmate** pulled him out of the smoke, and that he would never have survived otherwise.\n\nI asked the guide if the workers had ever complained. \"Of course,\" she said. \"They **worried** about their health and their pay. But they were also proud. Many said it was the hardest job in the **world**, and the best.\"\n\nBefore I left, I signed the visitors' book. I wanted to **write** something meaningful, but I could only manage a few lines. I wrote that the factory had shaped my family, and that I hoped we had not been **wrong** to let it go. Then I added: \"**Yes**, it was **worth** it.\" My father **would** have agreed. As I drove away, the guide waved. \"Don't **worry**,\" she called out. \"**You** can come back any time. There is **yet** more to discover.\""},"62":{"w":["your","yours","yourself","yourselves","yummy","zero","zip","zipper","zoo","zoom"],"title":"A Letter to the Zoo Volunteers","t":"Dear volunteers,\n\nThank you for **your** hard work at the city **zoo** this summer. When we started the programme in May, we had **zero** experience with children's tours. Now families **zoom** across the city every weekend to join them, and many ask for you by name.\n\nSeveral of you have told me that you surprised **yourselves**: shy students became confident guides, and one of you even learned to calm a nervous penguin. I hope each of you is proud of **yourself** as well as of the team.\n\nA few practical notes before the autumn season. Please check that the **zipper** on every first-aid bag works properly; two of them broke last month. When the snack stall gets busy, help the staff **zip** the cool boxes closed so the ice lasts longer. The new honey cakes, by the way, are **yummy**, but they are for visitors, not for us!\n\nFinally, the photo album from this summer is ready, and one copy is **yours** to keep.\n\nWith warm thanks,\nMs. Chen"}},"cet4":{"1":{"w":["abandon","ability","abnormal","aboard","absence","absent","absolute","absolutely","absorb","abstract","abundant","abuse","academic","academy","accelerate","acceleration","accent","accept","acceptable","acceptance"],"title":"A Mathematician Learns Chinese","t":"When Maria arrived in Shanghai to study at a language **academy**, she nearly decided to **abandon** the whole plan within a month. Her **academic** background was in mathematics, a world of **abstract** symbols and **absolute** rules, and the chaos of learning Chinese felt deeply **abnormal** to her. Every time she opened her mouth, her **accent** gave her away.\n\nHer teachers, however, refused to **accept** her pessimism. They explained that language learning rarely follows a straight line; progress tends to **accelerate** after a long, frustrating plateau. To prove the point, one teacher showed her a graph of a former student's test scores, which rose slowly for months and then climbed sharply, like a car gaining **acceleration** on an open road.\n\nMaria began to change her habits. She stopped treating mistakes as signs of a lack of **ability** and started to **absorb** the language from everything around her: street signs, conversations overheard **aboard** the metro, menus and songs. Opportunities to practise were **abundant**, she realised; she had simply been too afraid to use them. She also learned that an occasional **absence** from class was **acceptable**, but that being mentally **absent** while sitting in the room was far worse.\n\nNot everything was pleasant. Online, she once faced a wave of **abuse** from strangers who mocked her pronunciation. But her classmates rallied around her, and the experience taught her that **acceptance** in a new culture is earned slowly, through patience rather than perfection. A year later, she was **absolutely** certain she had made the right choice."},"2":{"w":["access","accessory","accident","accidental","accommodate","accommodation","accompany","accomplish","accord","accordance","accordingly","account","accumulate","accuracy","accurate","accuse","accustom","accustomed","ache","achieve"],"title":"An Accidental Career","t":"Few people plan to become hotel managers. For Chen Hao, it was entirely **accidental**. After a minor car **accident** forced him to give up his job as a delivery driver, he took a temporary position at the front desk of a small guesthouse, simply because it was the only work available. He did not expect to stay more than a month.\n\nYet he quickly became **accustomed** to the rhythm of the place. He learned how to **accommodate** guests with very different needs, from backpackers looking for cheap **accommodation** to elderly couples who wanted quiet rooms with easy **access** to the lift. He would **accompany** nervous first-time travellers to the bus station and help them **accomplish** small tasks, such as buying tickets or finding a pharmacy for a stubborn **ache**. Staff and guests were in complete **accord** that he was the friendliest person in the building.\n\nHis employer noticed his talent. In **accordance** with the guesthouse's policy of promoting from within, Chen was given more responsibility, and his salary rose **accordingly**. He began to **accumulate** knowledge about everything from booking systems to local history. Guests praised the **accuracy** of his advice; his directions were always **accurate**, and his restaurant suggestions rarely disappointed.\n\nNot every day was easy. Once a guest tried to **accuse** him of stealing a gold **accessory** that later turned up in her own handbag. Chen remained calm and polite, and the woman apologised. Over time he learned to **accustom** himself to such pressures. Ten years later he runs his own hotel. His greatest success, he says, was not something he planned to **achieve**; it was learning to say yes when life offered him an unexpected path. \"In my **account** of my life,\" he jokes, \"the accident is the hero.\""},"3":{"w":["achievement","acid","acknowledge","acquaintance","acquire","acquisition","acre","act","action","active","activity","actor","actress","actual","actually","acute","ad","adapt","add","addition"],"title":"A Second Life on Stage","t":"At forty-two, after twenty years in accounting, Wang Lin decided to **act** on a dream she had kept secret since childhood: she wanted to become an **actress**. Her friends thought it was a joke. An old **acquaintance** even asked whether she was having a midlife crisis.\n\nShe began by taking evening classes at a community theatre. The teacher, a retired **actor**, told the students that acting is not about pretending but about understanding. \"Every **action** on stage must have a reason,\" he said. Wang Lin found this surprisingly familiar; in accounting, too, every number had to be explained.\n\nThe work was demanding. She had to **acquire** new skills, from controlling her voice to moving naturally in front of an audience, and to **adapt** to a world in which emotion mattered more than precision. Her **acquisition** of these skills was slow at first, but her teacher **acknowledged|acknowledge** that she was one of the most **active** students in the group, always volunteering for extra rehearsals and every **activity** the theatre organised.\n\nHer first role was small: a farmer's wife in a play about a family who loses an **acre** of land to a factory. The play relied on a single sharp image to make its point, a jar of **acid** rainwater placed in the centre of the stage. When the curtain fell, the applause was **actually** louder than she had expected, and a local newspaper ran a small **ad** praising the production. For her, that applause was an **achievement** worth more than any promotion.\n\nHer **actual** income from acting is still tiny, so she keeps a part-time job. But she says the experience has given her something money cannot **add**: an **acute** awareness of how other people feel. In **addition**, she has discovered that it is never too late to begin a second life."},"4":{"w":["additional","address","adequate","adhere","adjective","adjust","administration","admire","admission","admit","adopt","adult","advance","advanced","advantage","adventure","adverb","advertise","advertisement","advice"],"title":"Learning to Write at University","t":"When I received my letter of **admission** to university, I was thrilled, but I soon had to **admit** that my English writing was far from **adequate**. My grammar was weak, and I still could not tell an **adjective** from an **adverb** without thinking hard.\n\nThe university **administration** offered **additional** classes for first-year students, and I signed up immediately. Our teacher, Professor Liu, was an energetic woman whom everyone came to **admire**. On the first day she gave us one piece of **advice**: \"**Adopt** the habits of good writers, not just their words.\" She asked us to read widely and to **adhere** to a simple rule: write something every day, even if it was only a paragraph.\n\nShe also encouraged us to treat writing as an **adventure**. One week she asked us to **advertise** an imaginary product; another week she made us write a formal **address** welcoming foreign visitors to the campus. I still remember the **advertisement** I wrote for a pair of shoes that could walk by themselves. It was ridiculous, but it taught me how much a single well-chosen word can change.\n\nSlowly, I began to **adjust** to university life. I discovered that being an **adult** learner had one great **advantage**: I could choose to learn for myself rather than for exams. By the end of the year my writing had made real **advances|advance**, and Professor Liu invited me to join her **advanced** seminar. It was the first time I had felt truly proud of my English."},"5":{"w":["advisable","advise","advocate","affair","affect","affection","affluent","afford","african","against","agency","agent","aggressive","agony","agriculture","aid","aim","aircraft","airline","airplane"],"title":"Medicine by Air","t":"Every year, a small charity **agency** based in Beijing sends medical **aid** to remote villages in East Africa. Its founder, Dr. Sun, started the project after a trip that changed his life. He had grown up in an **affluent** family and had never seen real poverty until he visited an **African** village where children were dying of treatable diseases.\n\nHe describes the scene he witnessed there as one of quiet **agony**: mothers walking for hours to reach a clinic that had no medicine. \"I could not **afford** to look away,\" he says. Today the charity works with a local **agent** who organises transport, and with an **airline** that donates spare cargo space on its regular flights. When roads are impassable, a small **aircraft** carries supplies directly to the villages; some of the older villagers had never seen an **airplane** land before.\n\nThe charity's **aim** is not only to treat illness but also to strengthen local **agriculture**, since poor nutrition **affects|affect** health more than any other single factor. Volunteers teach farmers how to protect crops **against** drought and pests, and they **advise** families on how to store food safely.\n\nDr. Sun is careful not to be too **aggressive** in promoting his own ideas. \"It is **advisable** to listen first,\" he says, \"and to **advocate** solutions that local people already believe in.\" Many villagers now regard him with genuine **affection**. When asked about his work, he insists that it is not his personal **affair** but a shared responsibility. \"Everyone who can help,\" he says, \"should.\""},"6":{"w":["alarm","alcohol","alert","algebra","alike","alive","allow","alloy","alone","aloud","alphabet","alter","alternative","although","altitude","altogether","aluminum","amaze","ambition","ambulance"],"title":"The Best Failure of My Life","t":"Last summer my cousin Jun, a maths teacher who usually spends his holidays marking **algebra** homework, set himself an unusual goal: to climb a mountain more than five thousand metres high. It was a long-held **ambition**, but his family reacted with **alarm**. His mother asked whether he would really climb **alone**; his wife quietly checked how far the nearest hospital was.\n\nJun prepared carefully. He gave up **alcohol**, trained every weekend and bought lightweight equipment, including walking poles made of an **aluminum** **alloy**. He also learned that at high **altitude** even fit climbers can fall ill, so he joined a small team led by an experienced guide who would **allow** no shortcuts.\n\n**Although** the first days went well, the weather changed suddenly on the fourth morning. A strong wind made it impossible to continue, and the guide had to **alter** the plan. Instead of pushing for the summit, the team took an **alternative** route along a lower ridge. Jun was disappointed, but the guide reminded them that the mountain would still be there next year; the important thing was to come home **alive**.\n\nThat evening, a climber from another group collapsed with altitude sickness, and an **ambulance** had to be called from the valley. The incident kept everyone **alert** for the rest of the trip. Jun later said that experienced and inexperienced climbers were **alike** in one way: nobody could predict how their body would react.\n\nHe never reached the top, but the journey still managed to **amaze** him. On the final night he read **aloud** from a small notebook in which he had written every Tibetan word he had learned, in order, like a child learning the **alphabet**. \"**Altogether**,\" he said, \"this was the best failure of my life.\""},"7":{"w":["amount","ampere","amplify","amuse","analysis","analyze","ancestor","anchor","ancient","angel","anger","angle","ankle","announce","announcer","annual","ant","anticipate","anxiety","anxious"],"title":"The Voice on the Radio","t":"For more than thirty years, Mr. Kang was the most familiar voice in his province. As a radio **announcer**, he would **announce** the morning news, introduce music programmes and, once a year, host the **annual** Spring Festival concert. Listeners trusted him like an old friend; some even called him \"the **angel** of the airwaves\".\n\nHis secret, he once explained, was preparation. Before every broadcast he would **analyze** each story carefully, asking himself what listeners would **anticipate** and what might surprise them. He believed that careful **analysis** could reveal an **angle** that others had missed. \"A good news programme does not just report facts,\" he said. \"It helps people understand them.\"\n\nHe was also known for his calm voice. Even when reporting on floods or accidents, he never let **anger** or **anxiety** creep into his tone. \"If listeners hear that the **anchor** is **anxious**, they will panic,\" he said. \"My job is to **amplify** the facts, not the fear.\"\n\nOutside work, he was a modest man with simple pleasures. He liked to **amuse** his grandchildren by reading stories in funny voices, and he spent weekends studying the history of his **ancestors|ancestor**, who had lived in the region since **ancient** times. He once spent a whole afternoon watching a line of **ants|ant** carry food across his garden path, and he later described the scene on air as a lesson in teamwork.\n\nHe retired after a fall in which he broke his **ankle**, but he still receives letters from listeners, and the **amount** of mail, he jokes, has hardly changed since his last broadcast. His old studio microphone ran on only a few **amperes|ampere** of current, he likes to point out, yet it reached millions of homes."},"8":{"w":["anyhow","anyone","anyway","anywhere","apart","apartment","apologize","apology","apparatus","apparent","appeal","appear","appearance","appetite","applause","appliance","applicable","applicant","application","apply"],"title":"The Job Interview","t":"Last spring my friend Lucy decided to **apply** for a job at a company that designs kitchen **appliances|appliance**. She had studied engineering but had little real experience, and she was worried that her **application** would be rejected **anyway**.\n\nThe company received more than five hundred **applicants|applicant**. Lucy was invited to an interview, and on the day she was so nervous that she had no **appetite** for breakfast. She arrived early and tried to look calm in the waiting room, though it was **apparent** to **anyone** watching that she was shaking.\n\nThe interview did not begin well. She accidentally knocked over a glass of water and had to **apologize** twice. But then something changed. The interviewers asked her to explain a strange piece of **apparatus** sitting on the table, a prototype toaster with sensors that could detect when bread was about to burn. Lucy forgot her nerves and began to describe how the design could be improved. Her ideas were simple but clearly **applicable** to real products.\n\nHer **appearance** might not have been perfect, but her enthusiasm made a strong **appeal** to the panel. One of them later told her that her honest **apology** had actually made a good impression; people who never make mistakes, he said, rarely learn much.\n\nA week later she got the job. She now lives in a small **apartment** near the office and says she could not imagine working **anywhere** else. Her colleagues **appear** to agree: at the end of her first project, the team gave her a round of **applause**. \"**Anyhow**,\" she says, laughing, \"I keep my drinks well **apart** from the prototypes these days.\""},"9":{"w":["appoint","appointment","appreciate","approach","appropriate","approval","approve","approximate","approximately","arbitrary","architect","architecture","area","argue","argument","arise","arithmetic","army","arouse","arrange"],"title":"Designing the New Library","t":"When the city council decided to build a new public library, it held a competition and invited architects from around the country to submit designs. The council would **appoint** a panel of judges, and the winning **architect** would be announced within six months.\n\nThe competition soon began to **arouse** strong feelings. Some residents wanted a modern glass building; others **argued|argue** that the city's traditional **architecture** should be respected. Public meetings were held in every **area** of the city, and each **argument** was carefully recorded. One elderly man complained that the choice of judges was **arbitrary**, but the council explained that each judge had been selected for a specific skill.\n\nThe winning design came from a young woman named Gao Yan. Her **approach** was to combine old and new: a building of local stone with large windows that let in natural light. Before construction could begin, she needed **approval** from the planning department, which had to **approve** every detail, from fire exits to the **approximate** number of books each floor could hold. The answer, she worked out with simple **arithmetic**, was **approximately** two hundred thousand.\n\nProblems continued to **arise** during construction. At one point heavy rain flooded the site, and soldiers from a nearby **army** base were sent to help pump out the water. Gao Yan had to **arrange** extra meetings almost every day.\n\nWhen the library finally opened, she said she was proud that the building felt **appropriate** to the city's history. She told reporters that she would always **appreciate** the residents who had shared their ideas, and she still keeps a notebook listing every **appointment** she had during those two busy years."},"10":{"w":["arrangement","arrest","arrival","arrow","art","article","artificial","artist","artistic","ascribe","ash","ashamed","aside","asleep","aspect","assemble","assembly","assess","assign","assignment"],"title":"Art from the Streets","t":"In our final year at secondary school, our **art** teacher gave the class an unusual **assignment**: each of us had to create a work of art from objects found on the street. She said she would **assess** our projects not on beauty but on imagination, and she would **assign** each student a different part of the city.\n\nMy **arrival** in my area, an old industrial district, was not encouraging. Most of the factories had closed, and the ground was covered with **ash** from a recent fire. At first I felt almost **ashamed** of how little I could find. But I decided to put my disappointment **aside** and look more carefully. Soon I had collected rusty nails, broken glass, a bicycle chain and a wooden **arrow** that had once been part of a sign.\n\nBack at school I tried to **assemble** these objects into a sculpture. I worked late into the night, and more than once my mother found me **asleep** on the floor of the garage. The **arrangement** I finally chose was simple: the arrow pointed upward from a nest of nails, as if a small factory were trying to rise again.\n\nWhen the projects were displayed at a school **assembly**, a local journalist wrote an **article** about them. One sculpture, made from **artificial** flowers and plastic bottles, caught the eye of a professional **artist**, who praised its **artistic** courage. My own piece did not win, but the journalist described it in her report, and the image of the rising arrow seemed to **arrest** the attention of readers.\n\nMany years later, I still think about that assignment, and I **ascribe** much of my interest in design to that teacher. She taught us that every **aspect** of a city, even its ruins, can tell a story."},"11":{"w":["assist","assistant","associate","association","assume","assumption","assure","astonish","astronaut","athlete","atmosphere","atmospheric","atom","atomic","attach","attack","attain","attempt","attend","attention"],"title":"A Lecture from Space","t":"When the space agency announced that a retired **astronaut** would give a public lecture at our university, the hall filled up within minutes. I was lucky: as a student **assistant** in the physics department, I had been asked to **assist** with the event, which meant I could **attend** for free.\n\nThe astronaut, a calm woman in her sixties, began by describing her training. Most people, she said, **assume** that astronauts are simply exceptional **athletes|athlete**, but that **assumption** is only half true. Physical fitness matters, but so does the ability to stay calm under pressure. She described an **attempt** to dock with a space station that nearly failed, and how her team managed to **attain** their goal by working slowly and checking every step.\n\nShe also talked about the Earth's **atmosphere**. Seen from orbit, she said, it looks astonishingly thin, like the skin of an apple. Many of the **atmospheric** changes that scientists worry about, from pollution to rising temperatures, are visible from space. She explained how the movement of every **atom** of oxygen and carbon is linked to life on the planet, and how **atomic** clocks on satellites help us measure these processes.\n\nSome students were surprised to learn that she had once been the target of an online **attack** after speaking out about climate change. She said she had learned not to **attach** too much importance to such comments. \"I can **assure** you,\" she told us, \"that the view from space makes small quarrels look very small indeed.\"\n\nAt the end, the president of the student science **association** thanked her and asked how ordinary people could help. She smiled and said that we should pay **attention** to the world around us and **associate** our daily choices with their long-term effects. Her honesty seemed to **astonish** the hall, and everyone rose to applaud."},"12":{"w":["attentive","attitude","attract","attraction","attractive","attribute","audience","audio","aural","author","authority","auto","autobiography","automatic","automatically","automation","automobile","auxiliary","available","avenue"],"title":"An Autobiography at Ninety","t":"Last year an elderly **author** in our city published her **autobiography** at the age of ninety. The book describes her childhood on a farm, her career as an engineer in the **automobile** industry and her late discovery of writing. To everyone's surprise, it became the most **attractive** book of the season for young readers.\n\nPart of its **attraction** lies in her **attitude**. She writes about failures and losses without bitterness, and she does not **attribute** her success to talent but to stubborn hard work. She also describes the arrival of **automation** in the factories where she worked, when machines began to perform **automatically** tasks that had once required dozens of workers. She admits that the change was painful for many families, but she argues that people adapted more quickly than anyone expected.\n\nWhen the publisher released an **audio** version of the book, read by the author herself, it reached an even wider **audience**. Many listeners said that her voice made the story more vivid; one reviewer described the recording as \"a rare **aural** pleasure\". Copies are now **available** in libraries across the country.\n\nHer success has made her a minor local **authority** on the history of the region. Schools invite her to speak, and she always asks for an **attentive** audience; if students look at their phones, she stops and waits. She refuses to use an **automatic** translation app when answering letters from abroad, preferring to look up each word in a dictionary.\n\nShe still drives an old **auto** she has owned for forty years, and she keeps an **auxiliary** battery in the boot in case it fails. Last month the city renamed a small street after her; the sign now reads \"Author's **Avenue**\". Her book continues to **attract** new readers every week."},"13":{"w":["average","aviation","avoid","await","awake","award","aware","awful","awfully","awkward","ax","axis","bachelor","background","backward","bacteria","badly","badminton","baggage","bake"],"title":"The Awkward Engineer","t":"My uncle is a forty-year-old **bachelor** who works as an engineer in the **aviation** industry. He is brilliant at mathematics; he can explain why a plane rotates around its vertical **axis** or how wind affects its flight. But in everyday life he is famously **awkward**.\n\nLast summer he decided to take up **badminton**, partly to lose weight and partly, I suspect, to meet new people. His first lesson went **badly**. He kept stepping **backward** at the wrong moment, missed almost every shot and finally fell over his own racket. \"That was **awful**,\" he admitted afterwards, laughing. \"I'm **awfully** glad nobody filmed it.\"\n\nBut he did not give up. He was **aware** that most beginners struggle, and he refused to **avoid** the courts just because he was embarrassed. He studied videos, practised every evening and gradually improved. His coach, who had a **background** in professional sport, said that his progress was well above **average** for someone his age.\n\nHe also changed other habits. He began to **bake** his own bread instead of buying cakes, and he learned that the **bacteria** in fermented foods could be good for his health. After a minor accident, he even replaced the old **ax** he used to chop firewood at his country cottage with a safer saw.\n\nSix months later, he entered a local amateur tournament. He did not win, but he received a small **award** for \"most improved player\". When he collected his **baggage** at the airport after a business trip the next week, he was still smiling about it. He told me that he used to lie **awake** at night worrying about failure; now he lies awake planning his next match. \"I'll **await** next year's tournament,\" he said, \"with my racket ready.\""},"14":{"w":["balance","balcony","balloon","ban","band","bang","banner","bar","barber","bare","barely","bargain","bark","barn","barrel","barrier","base","baseball","basic","basically"],"title":"Music in the Barn","t":"Every summer our village holds a music festival in an old wooden **barn** at the edge of the fields. It began twenty years ago, when a local **band** needed somewhere to rehearse and a farmer offered them his empty building. Today it attracts hundreds of visitors.\n\nThis year the organisers faced a problem. The regional government announced a **ban** on large outdoor events because of the risk of fire, and the festival committee had to **balance** safety against tradition. In the end they moved everything indoors and built a wooden **barrier** around the stage to keep the crowd at a safe distance.\n\nOn the opening night, the barn was decorated with a red **banner** and dozens of coloured **balloons|balloon**. The village **barber**, who plays the drums, opened the concert with a loud **bang** that made a dog outside begin to **bark**. A small **bar** in the corner sold lemonade and local beer from a wooden **barrel**, and children ran around in **bare** feet on the straw-covered floor.\n\nAt a **basic** level, the festival is simply a party, but for many villagers it means much more. It is the **base** of the community's identity, the one night of the year when everyone gathers in one place. \"**Basically**,\" said the farmer's grandson, \"it is the village's way of saying we are still here.\"\n\nTickets are cheap, a real **bargain** compared with city concerts, and the money is used to repair the barn. This year the committee **barely** covered its costs, but nobody complained. After the last song, a group of teenagers organised a game of **baseball** in the field under the stars, and the older guests watched from the **balcony** of the farmhouse until midnight."},"15":{"w":["basin","basis","bat","bath","bathe","bathroom","battery","battle","bay","beach","beam","bean","beard","beast","beauty","bee","beforehand","beg","beggar","beginner"],"title":"Summers by the Bay","t":"Every summer, my grandfather rents a small house by a quiet **bay** in the south. He has been going there for fifty years, and he still remembers the first time he saw the **beach**, a long curve of white sand framed by green hills.\n\nHe likes to prepare everything **beforehand**. Weeks before the trip he checks the **battery** in his old radio, buys a sack of dried **beans|bean** and packs a metal **basin** for washing fish. My grandmother laughs at him, but she admits that his planning has saved them more than once.\n\nLife at the bay is simple. In the morning he takes a cold **bath** in the sea; in the evening he likes to **bathe** his tired feet in warm water. The little cottage has no modern **bathroom**, only a shower behind the kitchen. At night a **bat** sometimes flies through the open window, and on calm evenings the **beam** of the lighthouse sweeps slowly across the water.\n\nHe is a gentle man with a long white **beard**, and children on the beach often follow him, asking for stories. His favourite is about a **beast** that supposedly lives in the deep water, a monster that, he admits, he invented to stop his own children from swimming too far out. Another tells of the **battle** fought between local fishermen and a great storm in 1962.\n\nHe also loves to teach. Last year he showed a **beginner** how to catch fish with a simple line, and he patiently explained how a **bee** finds its way back to the hive. Once, when a **beggar** came to the door, he invited the man in for dinner. \"Nobody should have to **beg** for food in a place of such **beauty**,\" he said. That evening, he insists, was the **basis** of a lasting friendship."},"16":{"w":["beginning","behalf","behave","behavior","being","belief","belong","beloved","belt","bench","bend","beneath","beneficial","benefit","berry","besides","bet","betray","beyond","bible"],"title":"The Bench Under the Cherry Tree","t":"In the park near my grandmother's home there is an old wooden **bench** **beneath** a cherry tree. Since the **beginning** of her retirement, she has sat there every morning, feeding the birds and watching the world go by. She says it is the most **beneficial** habit she has ever formed.\n\nThe bench has a history. A small brass plate on its back reads \"In memory of my **beloved** wife\", followed by a line from the **Bible**. My grandmother never knew the man who placed it there, but she feels that the bench now **belongs|belong** to her as much as to anyone.\n\nShe is a keen observer of human **behavior**. She notices how children **behave** when they think nobody is watching, how teenagers **bend** over their phones and how old men argue about football. She once made a **bet** with a neighbour about whether a shy young couple would ever speak to each other; she won, and the neighbour paid her with a jar of wild **berry** jam.\n\nHer **belief** is simple: every human **being** deserves a little kindness. She has helped lost tourists, comforted a crying child and once fixed a stranger's broken **belt** with a piece of string. She never expects thanks, and she says the **benefit** to herself is greater than any reward.\n\n**Besides** her park friends, she has very few visitors, but she does not feel lonely. \"Some people will **betray** your trust,\" she told me once, \"but most people are better than you expect.\" Last year, on **behalf** of the neighbourhood, the local council presented her with a small certificate. She keeps it in a drawer, but she says the real reward lies far **beyond** any piece of paper."},"17":{"w":["bicycle","bill","billion","bind","biology","birth","biscuit","bite","bitter","bitterly","blade","blame","blank","blanket","blast","blaze","bleed","blend","bless","blind"],"title":"Mr. Han's Lessons","t":"Mr. Han, my old **biology** teacher, rode the same **bicycle** to school for thirty years. He was a quiet man with a sharp sense of humour, and he liked to begin each lesson with a strange fact. \"The human body contains about thirty-seven trillion cells,\" he once announced. \"Not a million, not a **billion**, but trillions. Remember that the next time you feel small.\"\n\nHe believed that science should be felt, not just learned. When we studied plants, he showed us how to **blend** soil, sand and water to give seeds the best start, and he made each of us slice a leaf with a razor **blade** and examine it under a microscope. When we studied the senses, he asked us to taste a **bitter** herb and compare it with a sweet **biscuit**. On one winter morning, when the wind blew **bitterly**, he took us outside to show us how small animals survive under a **blanket** of snow.\n\nHe never liked to **blame** students for their mistakes. If someone handed in a **blank** sheet of paper, he would simply sit down with them and ask what they had not understood. Once, during a lesson on the circulation of blood, a boy cut his finger and began to **bleed**; Mr. Han calmly bandaged it and turned the accident into a lesson.\n\nHis own life was not easy. A terrible **blaze** caused by an electrical fault destroyed his house and his books, and the **blast** of an exploding gas bottle damaged his hearing. Later his eyesight weakened, and he feared he would go **blind**. Yet he never seemed resentful. He would often say that we should **bless** every day we are given.\n\nWhen he retired, our class paid the **bill** for a small party. He told us that teaching was the thread that would always **bind** us together, and that a teacher's words, like a mosquito's **bite**, can leave a mark long after the moment has passed. When my daughter's **birth** was announced last year, one of the first messages I received came from him."},"18":{"w":["blood","bloom","blossom","board","boast","boil","bold","bolt","bomb","bond","bone","boost","boot","booth","border","bore","born","bosom","boss","bottom"],"title":"The Border Market","t":"Every spring, when the apple trees **bloom** and the hills are covered in pink **blossom**, a small market opens near the **border** between two mountain provinces. Farmers from both sides set up a **booth** or two and sell everything from honey to leather **boots|boot**.\n\nThe market's **boss** is a cheerful woman named Mrs. Lei, who was **born** in the village and has organised the event for twenty years. She likes to **boast** that it is the oldest market in the region, and she may well be right. Its history is written on an old wooden **board** at the entrance, which lists every year the market has been held since 1950.\n\nMrs. Lei is **bold** in her ideas. She persuaded the local government to **boost** the market's budget so that farmers could build new stalls, and she started a programme to strengthen the **bond** between families on both sides of the border, who share a common history and, in many cases, the same **blood**. \"Politics may divide maps,\" she says, \"but not people.\"\n\nThe food is the main attraction. Visitors queue for soup made by slowly cooking a pig's **bone** for hours, letting the pot **boil** gently until the broth turns white. At the **bottom** of the hill, an old man sells hand-forged locks and an iron **bolt** that he swears will last a hundred years.\n\nThe market has had its dark moments. During a war in the last century, a **bomb** destroyed part of the village, and many families left. Some young people today find the market old-fashioned and say it can **bore** them within an hour. But for the older villagers, it is something they hold close to their **bosom**, a place full of shared memories."},"19":{"w":["bough","bounce","bound","boundary","bow","bowl","brain","brake","branch","brand","brandy","brass","brave","breadth","breast","breath","breathe","breed","breeze","brick"],"title":"The Band in the Square","t":"Every Sunday afternoon, a small **brass** band plays under the old oak tree in our town square. The musicians sit on folding chairs, and a gentle **breeze** carries the music across the fountain to the cafés on the other side. Children dance, dogs **bounce** around the square, and people of every age stop to listen.\n\nThe band was founded fifty years ago by a retired engineer who wanted to **breed** a love of music in the town. Its first rehearsals took place in a **brick** warehouse near the river. Today it has thirty members, from teenagers to a ninety-year-old trumpeter who still has the **breath** to play for two hours. \"When you play the trumpet, you learn to **breathe** properly,\" he says. \"It's better than any medicine.\"\n\nThe conductor, a **brave** young woman who took over three years ago, has widened the **breadth** of the band's music. Besides traditional marches, they now play film music, jazz and even pop songs. She says that music has no **boundary** and that every **branch** of it deserves respect. At the end of each concert, she turns to the audience and takes a deep **bow**.\n\nNot everyone welcomed the changes at first. Some older members felt **bound** by tradition and complained that the new pieces were too hard on the **brain**. But the crowds grew larger every month. A local coffee **brand** now sponsors the concerts, and a restaurant offers a free **bowl** of soup to every musician after the show.\n\nOn cold days, the old trumpeter keeps a small flask of **brandy** in his coat pocket, \"for the lungs,\" he insists. Once, a heavy **bough** from the oak tree fell during a storm and narrowly missed the drummer. Nobody was hurt. The drummer, who arrives every week on an old bicycle with a squeaking **brake**, joked that he had never moved so fast in his life. Then he placed a hand on his **breast** and laughed."},"20":{"w":["brief","brighten","brilliant","brim","brisk","bristle","brittle","broad","broadcast","broken","bronze","brood","brook","broom","brow","bruise","brute","bubble","bucket","bud"],"title":"Mornings by the Brook","t":"Every morning, my grandfather takes a **brisk** walk to the **brook** at the bottom of our garden. He carries a wooden **bucket** to collect water for his vegetables and a small **broom** to sweep the path. The walk is **brief**, no more than ten minutes, but he says it is the best part of his day.\n\nIn spring he watches for the first **bud** on the willow trees. He likes to point out the tiny **bubbles|bubble** rising from the stones on the bed of the stream, and the dragonflies whose wings shine like thin **bronze** plates in the sunlight. On a **brilliant** sunny day, the light seems to **brighten** everything, even his mood.\n\nHe is not a sentimental man. He spent forty years as an engineer, and he is quick to explain why old ice becomes **brittle** or why a **broad** river moves more slowly than a narrow stream. But he also has a gentle side. When a neighbour's cat came home with a deep **bruise** on its leg, he cleaned the wound and kept the animal warm for a week.\n\nSometimes he seems to **brood** over things. My grandmother says she can tell from his **brow**: when he is worried, it wrinkles like an old map. He worries about the way some people treat animals, and he once called a man who kicked a stray dog \"a **brute**\". The dog's fur, he remembers, would **bristle** whenever that man came near.\n\nEvery evening he listens to the news **broadcast** on his old radio, which has a **broken** dial but still works. \"Life is like water in a glass,\" he likes to say, filling his own to the **brim** at dinner. \"Pour slowly, and nothing spills.\""},"21":{"w":["budget","bulb","bulk","bull","bullet","bunch","bundle","burden","bureau","burn","burst","bury","bush","business","butcher","butter","butterfly","button","cabbage","cabin"],"title":"The Butcher on Our Street","t":"Mr. Wang has run the **butcher** shop on our street for thirty years. It is a small **business**, but it is the heart of the neighbourhood. Every morning he hangs a **bunch** of fresh herbs by the door and arranges cuts of meat, blocks of **butter** and heads of **cabbage** in neat rows on the counter.\n\nLast year the shop almost closed. A large supermarket opened nearby and began selling meat in **bulk** at very low prices. Mr. Wang's monthly **budget** no longer balanced, and the rent became a heavy **burden**. When the shop's lighting failed, he could not even afford to replace a single **bulb**. He applied for help from the city's small business **bureau**, but the paperwork took months.\n\nThen something unexpected happened. A local journalist wrote an article about him, describing him as \"as strong as a **bull** and as gentle as a **butterfly**\". The story spread online, and customers began to arrive from all over the city. Some brought him a **bundle** of old newspapers to wrap the meat in; others simply came to talk.\n\nMr. Wang admits that he was close to giving up. \"I felt my dreams were about to **burst** like a balloon,\" he said, \"and that I would have to **bury** thirty years of work.\" Instead, sales doubled. He hired an assistant, painted the shop and planted a small rose **bush** by the door.\n\nHe still works long hours, and last winter he **burned|burn** his hand badly on the grill. But he says he would not change his life. On the wall behind the counter hangs a photo of his father outside a wooden **cabin** in the mountains, next to a single brass **button** from his father's old coat. \"He used to say that hard times come at you as fast as a **bullet**,\" Mr. Wang says, \"and that the only answer is to stand your ground.\""},"22":{"w":["cabinet","cable","cafe","cafeteria","cage","cake","calculate","calculation","calculator","calendar","calm","camel","camera","camp","campaign","campus","canal","cancel","cancer","candidate"],"title":"The Campus Campaign","t":"When Lily decided to run for president of the student union, she knew it would be a difficult **campaign**. She was a second-year engineering student with no political experience, and the other **candidate** was a popular senior who had already served on the committee.\n\nShe began with a careful plan. She hung a large **calendar** on the wall of her dorm room, marked every event on **campus**, and used a **calculator** to work out how many students she could meet each day. Her **calculation** suggested that if she spoke to fifty people a day, she could reach almost everyone before the vote. Her friends teased her for trying to **calculate** everything, but she stayed **calm** and stuck to the plan.\n\nHer main promise was simple: to improve the student **cafeteria**, which served cold food and closed too early. She also wanted to open a small **cafe** in the library where students could study late. She used her **camera** to photograph empty shelves and broken chairs and shared the pictures online.\n\nHalfway through, she almost had to **cancel** everything. Her mother called to say that her grandfather had been diagnosed with **cancer**. Lily rushed home, but her grandfather insisted that she go back. \"A **camel** does not stop walking because the desert is long,\" he told her.\n\nWhen she returned, her supporters had set up a small **camp** outside the main building, with posters, a **cage** of paper birds symbolising freedom of choice and a homemade **cake** for everyone who voted. A **cable** television channel even interviewed her, and she promised to keep a filing **cabinet** of student complaints open to all.\n\nShe won by a narrow margin. Her first act as president was to organise a cleaning day along the old **canal** behind the university, a place everyone had forgotten."},"23":{"w":["candle","candy","cannon","canoe","canteen","canvas","cap","capable","capacity","capital","captain","captive","capture","carbon","career","careless","cargo","carpenter","carpet","carriage"],"title":"The Carpenter's Canoe","t":"My grandfather was a **carpenter** for fifty years, and the most remarkable thing he ever made was a wooden **canoe**. He built it slowly over one winter in a shed behind the house, working by the light of a **candle** whenever the power failed. My grandmother called it his foolish project, but she brought him tea and **candy** every evening anyway.\n\nHe had learned his trade as a young man, when he worked on a ship that carried **cargo** between ports along the coast. The **captain**, an old sailor with a loud voice, saw that the young man was **capable** of far more than cleaning decks and taught him to repair the wooden **carriage** of the ship's ancient **cannon**, a relic kept for decoration. From then on, my grandfather knew what he wanted for his **career**.\n\nHe was never **careless**. He measured each board twice, protected the floor of the shed with an old **carpet** and covered his work with a sheet of **canvas** at night. He even read about how different trees store **carbon**, because he wanted to use wood that had been grown sustainably. The finished canoe had the **capacity** to carry two adults and a child.\n\nThe next summer he took it to a lake near the **capital**, where he and my grandmother paddled across the water while a small crowd watched from the shore. A photographer managed to **capture** the moment, and the picture was later printed in a local newspaper. In it, my grandfather is wearing his favourite **cap** and laughing like a child.\n\nFor years that photo hung on the wall of the village **canteen**, where he ate lunch every day, and visitors still stop to look at it. Even in old age, he used to say, a man should never be a **captive** of routine."},"24":{"w":["carrier","carrot","cart","carve","cash","cassette","cast","castle","casual","catalog","cathedral","cattle","cause","cautious","cave","cease","ceiling","celebrate","cell","cellar"],"title":"A Week in a Cathedral Town","t":"Last autumn I spent a week in a small European town famous for its medieval **cathedral** and the ruins of a **castle** on the hill above it. I had chosen it almost at random from a travel **catalog** in a bookshop, and I expected a quiet, slightly boring trip.\n\nIt turned out to be one of the most memorable weeks of my life. On the first morning I joined a guided tour that began in the cathedral's **cellar**, a cold stone room where wine had once been stored. The guide explained that the town's wealth had come from **cattle** farming, and that the great painted **ceiling** of the cathedral had been paid for by farmers' donations. Craftsmen had spent decades learning to **carve** the wooden figures above the altar, and the bells had been **cast** in a local workshop.\n\nThe guide was a **casual**, friendly man who wore jeans and an old jumper. He told us that during a war in the seventeenth century, villagers had hidden in a **cave** outside town, waiting for the fighting to **cease**. When the danger passed, they returned and continued to **celebrate** their harvest festival, which is still held every October.\n\nI also discovered the town's market, where farmers sold everything from **carrot** cake to homemade cheese from a wooden **cart**. Most stalls accepted only **cash**, so I had to be **cautious** with my spending. One old man was even selling music on **cassette** tapes, which made me laugh; I had not seen one since childhood.\n\nOn my last evening I tried to call home, but my **cell** phone had no signal in the narrow old streets. Later I learned the **cause**: the thick stone walls. Instead, I handed a postcard to a postal **carrier** who promised it would reach China within two weeks. It arrived in three."},"25":{"w":["cement","cent","centigrade","centimetre","central","century","ceremony","certain","certainty","certificate","chain","chairman","chalk","challenge","chamber","champion","chance","channel","chaos","chapter"],"title":"The Youngest Champion","t":"At the age of eleven, Ming became the youngest **champion** in the history of our city's chess club. The club meets in a small **chamber** on the second floor of the town hall, a room that has hardly changed in a **century**; its **cement** floor has been worn smooth by generations of footsteps. On the wall hangs a framed **certificate** from 1925, the year the club was founded.\n\nMing did not seem to be a natural genius. When he first came to the club, the **chairman** told his mother that it was almost **certain** he would lose most of his games. But Ming had one quality that set him apart: he loved a **challenge**. Every time he lost, he went home, set up the board and replayed the game, move by move.\n\nHis coach, a retired maths teacher, wrote patterns on a blackboard with a piece of **chalk** and explained how each move was linked to the next like the links of a **chain**. \"Chess is not about luck or **chance**,\" he said, \"but about bringing order to **chaos**.\" He showed Ming how to control the **central** squares of the board and how to **channel** his energy into planning several moves ahead.\n\nThe final match of the championship ended with a small **ceremony** in the town hall. Ming played against a man in his fifties who had held the title for ten years. The temperature that afternoon was nearly thirty-five degrees **centigrade**, and the old fan in the corner barely moved the air. After four hours, Ming won by the narrowest of margins. The prize was a small trophy, barely ten **centimetres|centimetre** tall, and a coin worth one **cent**, painted gold, according to the club's oldest tradition.\n\nMing's mother says that this victory opened a new **chapter** in her son's life. He now studies with a coach online and hopes to compete nationally. One thing is a **certainty**: he will keep replaying every game he loses."},"26":{"w":["character","characteristic","characterize","charge","charity","charming","chart","chase","cheat","cheek","cheer","cheerful","cheese","chemical","chemist","chemistry","cheque","cherry","chess","chest"],"title":"The Charity Fair","t":"Every December our school holds a **charity** fair, and the star of the event is always Mr. Ross, the **chemistry** teacher. He is a **charming**, slightly eccentric man whose **character** seems to belong in an old novel. His most famous **characteristic** is a bright red bow tie, which he wears every day, even in summer.\n\nMr. Ross is in **charge** of the science stall. He trained as a **chemist** before becoming a teacher, and he uses simple **chemical** reactions to amaze visitors: he turns water purple, makes foam rise from a bottle and lets children dissolve sugar to see how crystals form. Parents **cheer** and children scream with delight.\n\nHis stall also sells food. He makes small sandwiches with **cheese** and **cherry** jam, a strange combination that has become a school tradition. Students **chase** each other around the hall, trying to be the first to buy one. Last year a boy tried to **cheat** by cutting in line, and Mr. Ross made him wait until the very end, then gave him an extra sandwich \"for patience\".\n\nHe keeps a **chart** on the wall showing how much money the fair has raised each year, from a few hundred yuan in the first year to more than twenty thousand last year. The money goes to a children's hospital, and every year the hospital director comes to collect a **cheque** and thank the students in person.\n\nOutside the stall, Mr. Ross sets up a giant **chess** board where parents and children play against each other, and visitors describe the atmosphere as warm and **cheerful**. If you had to **characterize** the fair in one image, it would be Mr. Ross at the end of the day, with flour on his **cheek** and a hand pressed to his **chest**, laughing at the final total."},"27":{"w":["chew","chicken","chief","childhood","childish","chill","chimney","chin","chocolate","choice","choke","choose","chop","church","cigarette","circle","circuit","circular","circulate","circumference"],"title":"Grandmother's House","t":"When I think of my **childhood**, I think of my grandmother's old house in the countryside. It had a crooked **chimney**, a yard full of **chickens|chicken** and a kitchen that always smelled of wood smoke. On winter mornings a **chill** would creep through the windows, and my grandmother would wrap me in a blanket and give me a cup of hot **chocolate**.\n\nShe was the **chief** cook of the family and took the job seriously. She could **chop** vegetables with astonishing speed, and she insisted that I **chew** every mouthful slowly. \"Your stomach has no teeth,\" she would say, tapping my **chin** with her finger. Once I tried to swallow a large piece of meat too quickly and began to **choke**; she calmly patted my back until it came out, then laughed at my **childish** pride.\n\nOn Sundays she took me to the small **church** at the edge of the village, not because she was very religious but because she loved the music. Afterwards we walked home in a wide **circle** through the fields, and she taught me the name of every plant. She also explained how electricity travels around a **circuit**, using a battery and a bulb she kept in a drawer, and how blood **circulates|circulate** through the body.\n\nMy grandfather, meanwhile, smoked a single **cigarette** every evening on the porch, a habit she disliked but tolerated. He liked to measure things: the **circumference** of the old oak tree, the height of the wheat. He once drew a **circular** map of the village on the back of an envelope.\n\nWhen I was ten, my parents gave me a **choice**: to stay in the city for the summer or to spend it in the countryside. I did not hesitate to **choose** the old house. It was the best decision of my young life."},"28":{"w":["circumstance","citizen","civil","civilian","civilization","civilize","claim","clap","clarify","clasp","classic","classical","classification","classify","classmate","claw","clay","clearly","clerk","clever"],"title":"The Museum Clerk","t":"In a small museum in the centre of our city, a **clerk** named Mrs. Zhou has spent twenty years trying to **classify** objects from the region's past. Her work is quiet and detailed: each pot, coin and letter must be described, photographed and placed in the right category. The **classification** system she designed is now used by museums across the province.\n\nMrs. Zhou is a **clever** woman with a dry sense of humour. She likes to say that every object tells a story about the **circumstance** in which it was made. A **clay** bowl, for example, can reveal what people ate, how they cooked and even what kind of **civilization** they belonged to. Some of the pots still carry the marks of an animal's **claw**, left when a cat walked across the wet clay thousands of years ago.\n\nHer favourite collection consists of letters written by ordinary **citizens|citizen** during a **civil** war more than a century ago. Most of the writers were **civilians|civilian**, not soldiers: farmers, teachers and shopkeepers who described their fears and hopes with surprising honesty. \"We often **claim** that history is about kings and battles,\" she says, \"but it is really about people like these.\"\n\nShe has also helped schools use the museum to teach history. She invites students to read **classic** texts alongside the objects and to listen to **classical** music from the same period. One former student, now a teacher herself, says that Mrs. Zhou helped her see the past **clearly** for the first time. \"She never tried to **civilize** us,\" the student laughed. \"She just made us curious.\"\n\nWhen Mrs. Zhou retired last year, the director gave a speech to **clarify** how much she had done for the museum, and the audience began to **clap** for several minutes. My mother, her old **classmate** from school, stood beside her and held her hand in a tight **clasp**."},"29":{"w":["cliff","climate","climb","cloak","clock","closely","cloth","clothe","clothing","club","clue","clumsy","coach","coal","coarse","coast","coat","cock","code","coil"],"title":"The Climbing Club","t":"Every Saturday morning, members of our town's climbing **club** meet at the bus station and travel together to the **coast**. Their goal is a tall white **cliff** above the sea, popular with climbers because of its firm rock and beautiful views. The **climate** there is mild, but the wind can be fierce, so everyone brings a warm **coat**.\n\nThe club's **coach**, a former mountain guide, insists that safety comes first. Before each **climb** he checks every rope and every **coil** of wire, and he watches each beginner **closely**. He has developed a simple **code** of hand signals so that climbers can communicate even when the wind makes it impossible to hear. \"If you're **clumsy** on the ground,\" he jokes, \"you'll be clumsy on the rock.\"\n\nThe coach is also a keen historian. He tells new members that two hundred years ago the cliffs were used by smugglers who wore a dark **cloak** to hide in the shadows. Ships carried **coal** along the coast, and the old harbour wall still has rusty iron rings where boats were tied. On one climb, a member found an old silver button wedged in a crack: a **clue**, perhaps, to a smuggler's escape.\n\nMembers bring their own **clothing**, but the club provides gloves made of a **coarse** **cloth** that grips the rock well. The club also raises money to feed and **clothe** homeless people in town during the winter.\n\nAt the end of each trip, the group returns to a seaside café, where an old **clock** above the counter stopped long ago at exactly half past four. The owner keeps a noisy **cock** in the back garden, which crows whenever a bus arrives, whatever the time of day."},"30":{"w":["coin","cold","collaborate","collapse","collar","colleague","collect","collection","collective","collision","colonel","colony","color","column","comb","combination","combine","comedy","comfort","comfortable"],"title":"A Colleague's Coins","t":"My **colleague** Mr. Tian has an unusual hobby: he collects old coins. His **collection** includes more than three thousand pieces from fifty countries, and he keeps them in neat rows in a glass cabinet in his office. Each **coin** has a small label describing its origin, its date and the **color** of its metal.\n\nHe began to **collect** coins as a child, after his grandfather, a retired army **colonel**, gave him a silver dollar from the 1930s. The colonel had served in several countries, including a former British **colony** in Southeast Asia, and he had brought home coins from every place he visited. \"Every coin is a tiny history book,\" he told his grandson.\n\nMr. Tian believes that collecting is a **collective** activity. He belongs to a club of collectors who meet every month to swap pieces and share knowledge. They often **collaborate** with local museums, and last year they helped to **combine** two private collections into a public exhibition. The **combination** of rare coins and old photographs attracted thousands of visitors.\n\nNot everything has gone smoothly. Once, a shelf holding part of his collection began to **collapse** after a minor **collision** with a cleaning trolley, and dozens of coins rolled across the floor. Mr. Tian spent the whole afternoon on his knees, trying to **comb** the carpet for every missing piece. The rest of us laughed, and the scene became a kind of office **comedy**.\n\nHe also writes a weekly **column** about coins for a local newspaper. On **cold** winter evenings he sits in his **comfortable** armchair, polishing coins and listening to the radio; he says the hobby brings him **comfort** after a stressful day. Even his dog, whose **collar** is decorated with tiny coins, seems to enjoy it."},"31":{"w":["command","commander","comment","commerce","commercial","commission","commit","commitment","committee","common","commonly","communicate","communication","communism","communist","community","companion","comparable","comparative","comparatively"],"title":"Saving the Park","t":"When the city government announced a plan to build a shopping centre on the site of our neighbourhood park, residents were quick to **comment**. Within a week, a **committee** of volunteers had formed to **communicate** their concerns to the authorities. Its leader was Mr. Fang, a retired army **commander** who still spoke with the quiet authority of someone used to giving a **command**.\n\nMr. Fang believed that **communication** was the key. He organised public meetings, wrote letters to the newspapers and invited the city planning **commission** to visit the park. He argued that **commerce** was important, but that a **community** also needed green spaces where people could meet. \"A **commercial** centre can be built anywhere,\" he said. \"A hundred-year-old tree cannot.\"\n\nThe members had grown up in very different times. Some older members remembered the early years of **communism**, when every factory had its own park and sports ground; one former **communist** official recalled that public spaces had been considered a basic right. Younger members, by contrast, were **commonly** more interested in cafés and bike lanes. Yet they all shared a **common** goal.\n\nMr. Fang asked each member to **commit** at least two hours a week to the campaign, and their **commitment** impressed even the planners. After months of negotiation, the city agreed to use a **comparable** site for the shopping centre, a former car park only a kilometre away, at a **comparatively** small extra cost.\n\nToday the park remains, and Mr. Fang still walks there every morning with his faithful **companion**, a white-haired dog named General. A university later used the case in a **comparative** study of urban planning in Chinese cities."},"32":{"w":["compare","comparison","compass","compel","compete","competent","competition","competitive","compile","complain","complaint","complete","completely","complex","complicate","complicated","component","compose","composition","compound"],"title":"The Composition Competition","t":"Every year our university holds a music **competition** for student composers. The rules are simple: each entrant must **compose** an original piece of no more than ten minutes for a small orchestra. Last year my roommate Jia decided to **compete** for the first time.\n\nShe was a **competent** pianist but had never written a full **composition**, and at first she found the task **complicated**. A piece of music, she explained, is like a chemical **compound**: each **component**, from melody to rhythm, affects all the others. Change one element and you may **complicate** the whole structure.\n\nShe worked late every night, and our room was soon full of scribbled notes. She tried to **compare** her work with famous pieces, but the **comparison** only made her feel worse. \"I can't **complete** anything,\" she would **complain**, throwing pages on the floor. Her main **complaint** was that she had no **compass** to guide her through such a **complex** process.\n\nOur music teacher suggested that she stop listening to other composers for a while. \"Nobody can **compel** inspiration,\" he said. \"**Compile** a notebook of small ideas instead, and see which ones grow.\" Jia followed his advice, and slowly the piece began to take shape.\n\nThe atmosphere on the day of the final was intensely **competitive**, with twenty entrants from across the country. Jia did not win first prize, but her piece came third, and the judges praised it for being **completely** original. Afterwards she told me that the real victory had been finishing at all."},"33":{"w":["comprehension","comprehensive","compress","comprise","compromise","compute","comrade","conceal","concentrate","concentration","concept","concern","concerning","concert","conclude","conclusion","concrete","condemn","condense","condition"],"title":"The Art of Concentration","t":"Many students believe that the secret of success is simply hard work. Professor Ding, who teaches reading **comprehension** at our university, disagrees. In her view, the key **concept** is **concentration**: the ability to **concentrate** on one task for a long time without distraction.\n\nHer course is **comprehensive**. It **comprises|comprise** lectures, discussions and weekly reading tests, and students are often asked to **condense** a long article into a single paragraph. \"If you can **compress** an idea without losing its meaning,\" she says, \"you truly understand it.\"\n\nShe is open about her **concern** that smartphones have damaged young people's attention. She does not **condemn** technology; she uses it herself to **compute** reading speeds and test results. But she asks students not to **conceal** their phones under their desks. Instead, everyone places their phone in a box at the front of the room. Some students found this rule unfair at first, but after a few weeks most accepted it as a reasonable **compromise**.\n\nHer **conclusion**, based on years of teaching, is clear: students who read in silence for thirty minutes a day improve faster than those who study for hours with constant interruptions. She **concludes|conclude** each semester with a reading **concert**, in which students read favourite passages aloud while the others listen.\n\nWhen I asked for her advice **concerning** exam preparation, she gave a very **concrete** answer: sleep well, read every day and treat each classmate as a **comrade** rather than a rival. Under the right **conditions|condition**, she said, anyone can learn to read deeply."},"34":{"w":["conduct","conductor","conference","confess","confidence","confident","confine","confirm","conflict","confuse","confusion","congratulate","congratulation","congress","conjunction","connect","connection","conquer","conquest","conscience"],"title":"The Conductor's Confession","t":"At an international music **conference** in Shanghai last year, the famous **conductor** Li Wen surprised his audience by deciding to **confess** a secret: for the first ten years of his career, he had almost no **confidence** in himself. \"Every time I stood in front of an orchestra,\" he said, \"I was afraid they would discover I didn't know what I was doing.\"\n\nHis early years were full of **conflict**. Critics described his style as strange, and some musicians openly questioned his decisions. The **confusion** was partly his own fault; he tried to please everyone and ended up satisfying no one. He kept changing his mind during rehearsals, which would **confuse** the players.\n\nThings changed when an old teacher told him to stop trying to **conquer** the music and simply listen to it. \"Your job is to **conduct**, not to control,\" the teacher said. \"Your only judge should be your **conscience**.\" Li Wen began to treat each concert as a conversation rather than a **conquest**. He learned to **connect** with each musician individually and to see the **connection** between his own feelings and the sound of the orchestra.\n\nSlowly he became more **confident**. A decade later he was invited to perform at a gathering of the national **congress**, and the event helped to **confirm** his reputation. Today he refuses to **confine** himself to one style, performing everything from Bach to film music.\n\nAfter his speech, dozens of young musicians lined up to **congratulate** him. One student handed him a card that read simply, \"**Congratulations|congratulation** on your honesty.\" He laughed and said that, like a **conjunction** in a sentence, a conductor's role is to join things together, not to stand out."},"35":{"w":["conscious","consciousness","consent","consequence","consequently","conservation","conserve","consider","considerable","considerate","consideration","consist","consistent","constant","constitution","construct","construction","consult","consume","consumer"],"title":"Small Habits, Big Consequences","t":"Every **consumer** makes choices that affect the planet, often without being fully **conscious** of them. This was the main message of a public lecture on environmental **conservation** that I attended last month. The speaker, a biologist named Dr. Hu, argued that the problem is not a lack of information but a lack of **consciousness**.\n\nShe began with a simple example. A single cup of coffee, she explained, may **consume** dozens of litres of water before it reaches your hands, if you **consider** the whole process of growing, roasting and transporting the beans. The **consequence** is that small daily habits add up to a **considerable** impact. **Consequently**, she said, people who want to **conserve** resources should start with ordinary routines rather than grand gestures.\n\nDr. Hu was **considerate** towards her audience. She did not blame anyone; she simply asked us to take her points into **consideration**. Real change, she said, must **consist** of many small steps, and those steps must be **consistent**: **constant** small efforts matter more than occasional heroic ones.\n\nShe also discussed the role of law. In some countries, she noted, the **constitution** now includes the right to a healthy environment, and governments must **consult** citizens before approving major projects. She showed photos of a new **construction** project near a wetland, where engineers had to **construct** special bridges so that animals could cross safely.\n\nAt the end, a student asked whether governments should ever act without public **consent**. Dr. Hu smiled and said that real progress happens only when people agree to change. \"Laws can guide us,\" she said, \"but habits change the world.\""},"36":{"w":["consumption","contact","contain","container","contemporary","contempt","content","contest","continent","continual","continue","continuous","contract","contradiction","contrary","contrast","contribute","contribution","control","convenience"],"title":"The Container Library","t":"In a small coastal town, an old shipping **container** has been turned into a public library. The idea came from a local teacher, Ms. Bai, who noticed that the town's children had little **contact** with books outside school. Instead of complaining, she decided to **contribute** something herself.\n\nShe signed a **contract** with the port authority to rent an unused container for a symbolic fee. Volunteers painted it bright blue, built shelves and installed solar panels to **control** the temperature inside. The finished library can **contain** about two thousand books, from **contemporary** novels to atlases of every **continent**.\n\nAt first, some residents treated the project with **contempt**, calling it a waste of time. In **contrast**, the children loved it from the first day. Ms. Bai organised a reading **contest** every month, and the **content** of the shelves changed **continually|continual** as people donated books. The library's popularity seemed to be in direct **contradiction** with the common belief that young people no longer read.\n\nThe project has changed adults' habits too. Parents who once spent hours scrolling on their phones now borrow books for themselves. Ms. Bai keeps a record of book **consumption** in the town, and the numbers **continue** to rise. \"Reading is like learning to swim,\" she says. \"Once you start, the movement becomes **continuous**.\"\n\n**Contrary** to her fears, the library has never been damaged by vandals. Its greatest **contribution**, she believes, is not the books themselves but the simple **convenience** of having them close to home."},"37":{"w":["convenient","convention","conventional","conversely","conversion","convert","convey","convince","cooperate","coordinate","cope","copper","cord","cordial","core","corn","corporation","correct","correction","correspond"],"title":"New Life for the Copper Works","t":"For decades the **copper** factory on the edge of our city was the town's economic **core**. When it closed, many people feared the area would decline. But five years later, a local **corporation** decided to **convert** the old buildings into a creative centre for artists, designers and small businesses.\n\nThe **conversion** was not easy. The project manager had to **coordinate** the work of architects, engineers and hundreds of builders, and **convince** sceptical residents that the plan would benefit them. He held public meetings every month to **convey** the latest progress and answer questions. \"We cannot **cope** with every problem alone,\" he admitted. \"We need people to **cooperate**.\"\n\nThe final design mixed the old and the new. The original brick walls were kept, but each workshop was fitted with modern lighting, and every old power **cord** was replaced. A large hall now hosts an annual design **convention**, attracting visitors from across the country. **Conversely**, the small studios upstairs remain quiet and private.\n\nResidents found the centre surprisingly **convenient**. Families come at weekends to buy handmade goods, and farmers sell fresh **corn** and vegetables at a weekly market in the courtyard. The atmosphere is relaxed and **cordial**, very different from the **conventional** shopping malls downtown.\n\nSome critics say the project has made the area too expensive. The manager accepts that mistakes were made and says the corporation will **correct** them; rents for local artists have already been reduced as a **correction**. Whether the centre will truly **correspond** to the community's needs, he says, will become clear only with time."},"38":{"w":["correspondent","corresponding","corridor","costly","cottage","cotton","cough","council","count","counter","county","couple","courage","course","court","cousin","cow","coward","crack","craft"],"title":"The Correspondent Retires","t":"After thirty years as a foreign **correspondent**, Mr. Gao retired to a small **cottage** in the countryside of a southern **county**. He had reported from war zones and summit meetings, but he says the bravest decision of his life was to leave the city and start again.\n\nThe cottage was old and **costly** to repair. Its narrow **corridor** leaked when it rained, and one wall showed a long **crack** that ran from floor to ceiling. Mr. Gao learned the **craft** of plastering from a local builder, and slowly the house became comfortable. He planted **cotton** in the garden, partly as an experiment, and bought a **cow** from a neighbour, though he admits he has no idea how to milk it.\n\nHis **cousin**, a retired judge from the city **court**, visits him every summer. The two men sit by the kitchen **counter** in the evenings, arguing about politics and laughing at old stories. Whenever they disagree, Mr. Gao's wife reminds them that they are a **couple** of stubborn old men.\n\nThe local **council** asked him to write a column for the county newspaper. He agreed on one condition: he would write about ordinary people. His articles describe farmers, teachers and shopkeepers, and every story comes with a **corresponding** photograph. Readers say they **count** among the most honest pieces in the paper.\n\nMr. Gao believes that real **courage** is not the absence of fear. He has met soldiers who felt no fear at all and **cowards|coward** who were brave when it mattered. \"Every life is a long **course**,\" he says, \"and nobody gets through it without a few bruises.\" He still has a dry **cough** from years on dusty roads, but he says he has never felt healthier."},"39":{"w":["crane","crash","crawl","crazy","cream","create","creative","creature","creep","crew","cricket","crime","criminal","cripple","crisis","critic","critical","criticism","criticize","crop"],"title":"The Night the Crane Fell","t":"At three o'clock one morning, a construction **crane** on the edge of our city collapsed with a terrible **crash**. Nobody was hurt, but the accident caused a **crisis** for the company that owned it, and the local media began to **criticize** its safety record.\n\nRumours spread quickly. Some people claimed that a **criminal** gang had damaged the crane on purpose; others insisted that it had simply been poorly maintained. The police investigated for a week but found no evidence of a **crime**. Instead, engineers discovered that a steel bolt had been cracked for months, and that workers had reported it without anyone taking action.\n\nThe company's director at first rejected the **criticism**, calling the reports **crazy**. But one newspaper **critic** wrote a sharp article describing how cost-cutting had begun to **cripple** the firm's maintenance department. The article proved **critical**: within days the director resigned, and a new team was appointed.\n\nThe new manager, a former engineer, took a more **creative** approach. She invited workers to suggest improvements and set up a system under which any **crew** member could stop work if they saw danger. \"We need to **create** a culture of honesty,\" she said. \"Problems should never be allowed to **creep** in quietly.\"\n\nLife on the site slowly returned to normal. The workers even started a weekly game of **cricket** at lunchtime, a sport the new manager had learned while studying abroad. Nearby farmers, whose **crop** of vegetables had been flattened by the fallen crane, received compensation. One farmer joked that the only **creature** truly frightened by the crash was his cat, which refused to **crawl** out from under the bed for two days. To celebrate the reopening, the manager brought cakes topped with fresh **cream** for every worker."},"40":{"w":["cross","crow","crowd","crown","crucial","crude","cruel","crush","crust","crystal","cube","cubic","cucumber","cue","cultivate","culture","cunning","cupboard","cure","curiosity"],"title":"The Crystal Shop","t":"In the old part of the city there is a tiny shop that sells **crystals|crystal**, minerals and fossils. Its owner, Mrs. Ning, has run it for forty years, and her **curiosity** about the natural world seems endless. She can explain how a perfect **cube** of salt forms, or how to calculate the **cubic** volume of a rock sample, as easily as other people discuss the weather.\n\nThe shop attracts a strange **crowd**: geologists, children, artists and the occasional tourist who wanders in by accident. Mrs. Ning treats them all with the same warm patience. She keeps her rarest pieces in an old wooden **cupboard** behind the counter and brings them out only when she senses genuine interest. \"People need a **cue**,\" she says. \"When their eyes light up, I know they are ready.\"\n\nHer most famous object is a **crude** lump of grey rock that hides a glittering **crust** of tiny crystals inside; she calls it the **crown** of her collection. She found it as a girl on a mountain path, while a **crow** watched from a tree, and she says it taught her that beauty is often hidden beneath rough surfaces.\n\nMrs. Ning believes that schools should **cultivate** children's interest in science from an early age. In her view, a society's **culture** depends on its willingness to ask questions. She has little patience for **cunning** dealers who pass off fake stones as real, and she considers it **cruel** to sell children worthless objects at high prices.\n\nHer advice for anyone who wants to **cross** from curiosity to real knowledge is **crucial**: never **crush** a question, however strange it seems. She admits that collecting rocks will not **cure** loneliness, but she says it helps. And at lunchtime she always eats the same thing: a simple sandwich of bread and **cucumber**."},"41":{"w":["curious","curl","current","curse","curtain","curve","cushion","custom","customer","cycle","daily","dairy","dam","damage","damp","danger","dangerous","dare","daring","darling"],"title":"The Dairy by the Dam","t":"Near the old **dam** outside our town there is a small **dairy** farm run by a family that has kept cows for four generations. Its **customer** base is small but loyal: local cafés, a few schools and families who drive out every weekend to buy fresh milk, cheese and yoghurt.\n\nThe farm's owner, a **daring** young woman named Su Yan, took over from her father five years ago. She was **curious** about modern methods and wanted to break with **custom**. Instead of selling to a big company at low prices, she opened a farm shop with soft **cushion** seats and checked **curtains|curtain**, where visitors can taste the products. \"If you **dare** to try something new,\" she says, \"people notice.\"\n\nRunning the farm is not easy. Milk production follows a natural **cycle**, and her **daily** routine begins at four in the morning. The fields near the river are often **damp**, which can be **dangerous** for the animals' health. Last spring a flood caused serious **damage** to the barn, and the **current** was so strong that it swept away part of the fence.\n\nSome neighbours warned her of the **danger** of taking on too much risk, and one old farmer said she must be under a **curse** when the bank refused her a loan. \"There is no such thing as bad luck,\" she replied. \"There's only bad planning.\"\n\nToday the farm is doing well. Visitors love watching the calves **curl** up in the straw, and the road to the farm, with its long **curve** around the hill, has become a popular cycling route. Her father, now retired, still calls her \"my **darling**\" in front of customers, which embarrasses her terribly."},"42":{"w":["dash","data","dawn","daylight","deadly","deaf","deal","death","debate","debt","decade","decay","deceit","deceive","decent","decision","deck","declare","decline","decorate"],"title":"Ten Years at Sea","t":"When my grandfather was young, he worked for a **decade** on a cargo ship that sailed between Shanghai and the Pacific islands. He still remembers standing on the **deck** at **dawn**, watching the first **daylight** spread across the sea. \"Those were the best years of my life,\" he says, \"and the most dangerous.\"\n\nThe work was hard and sometimes **deadly**. During one storm, a sailor was swept overboard, and my grandfather witnessed his first **death**. He says the experience changed him; from that moment he made a **decision** never to take life for granted.\n\nHe also learned about honesty. One day the captain discovered that a crew member had tried to **deceive** him by hiding stolen goods in the cargo hold. The captain was furious, not only about the theft but about the **deceit**. \"A ship survives on trust,\" he told the crew. He made the man **declare** everything he had taken and pay back the **debt** from his wages.\n\nMy grandfather left the sea after ten years to start a family. He found a **decent** job in a shipyard, where he helped **decorate** the interiors of passenger ships. The shipbuilding industry began to **decline** in the 1990s, and many yards fell into **decay**, but he kept his job until retirement.\n\nNow in his eighties, he is slightly **deaf**, but he loves to **debate** history with his grandchildren. He still keeps the **data** from his voyages, notebooks full of weather readings and distances, and every evening he makes a **dash** to the window to watch the sunset. \"Life has given me a good **deal**,\" he says."},"43":{"w":["decrease","deduce","deed","deepen","deer","defeat","defect","defence","defend","define","definite","definitely","definition","degree","delay","delegate","delegation","delete","deliberate","deliberately"],"title":"The Delegation to Geneva","t":"Last spring our university sent a **delegation** of ten students to an international conference on environmental policy in Geneva. Each **delegate** had to prepare a speech that would **define** a clear position on climate change and **defend** it against criticism.\n\nOur team leader, Professor Wu, insisted that every argument be supported by evidence. \"Do not **deduce** conclusions from feelings,\" she told us. \"Begin with a precise **definition** of the problem.\" She made us **delete** every sentence that was vague or emotional, and she was **definite** about one thing: we would not exaggerate.\n\nBad weather held up our flight for six hours, and the **delay** left us only one evening to rehearse. We worked late into the night. One teammate, a quiet engineering student, realised that our data contained a serious **defect**: the figures for carbon emissions had been entered in the wrong units. If he had not noticed, our whole presentation would have been ruined.\n\nAt the conference our speeches were well received. One rival team **deliberately** tried to confuse us with difficult questions, but we answered calmly. Since it was not a competition, we did not **defeat** anyone, but we earned the respect of many participants. One judge described our **defence** of reducing plastic use as \"a careful and **deliberate** argument\".\n\nThe experience helped to **deepen** my interest in environmental law, and I am now working towards a **degree** in the field. I also learned that the world will not **decrease** its emissions without countless small good **deeds|deed**. On our last day we walked in a forest outside the city and saw a **deer** standing silently among the trees. I will **definitely** go back."},"44":{"w":["delicate","delicious","delight","deliver","delivery","demand","democracy","democratic","demonstrate","dense","density","deny","depart","department","departure","depend","dependent","deposit","depress","depression"],"title":"Behind Every Parcel","t":"When my cousin Yue graduated, she took a job in the **delivery** **department** of a large online shop. She expected a simple office job, but she soon discovered that her work was at the centre of the company's success.\n\nEvery day millions of customers **demand** fast service, and her department must **deliver** parcels to cities across the country within forty-eight hours. Yue's task is to plan routes in areas with a high population **density**, where traffic is **dense** and drivers struggle to find parking. She uses data to **demonstrate** which routes save the most time.\n\nThe job can be stressful. In the busiest season the pressure can **depress** even the most cheerful staff, and one of her colleagues took sick leave for **depression**. Yue believes the company should **depend** less on overtime and more on better planning. She does not **deny** that the work is hard, but she says the managers have started to listen.\n\nHer department also handles fragile goods. Yue once supervised the **departure** of a truck carrying **delicate** glass vases to an exhibition. Every box had to be packed with care, and the customer paid a **deposit** for special insurance. When the vases arrived unbroken, the customer sent the team a box of **delicious** cakes, to everyone's **delight**.\n\nYue says her job has shown her how **dependent** modern society is on invisible workers. She also believes that workplaces should be more **democratic**, giving ordinary staff a voice in decisions. \"**Democracy** starts with small things,\" she says, \"like asking the drivers before you change their routes.\" Before the trucks **depart** each morning, she walks around the yard to say hello to every driver."},"45":{"w":["depth","deputy","derive","descend","describe","description","desert","deserve","design","desirable","desire","despair","desperate","despise","despite","destination","destroy","destruction","destructive","detail"],"title":"Into the Canyon","t":"Last summer a team of geologists set out to explore a remote **desert** in western China. Their **destination** was a deep canyon that had never been fully mapped. The team leader, Dr. Ma, and his **deputy**, a young scientist named Lin, spent months planning every **detail** of the journey.\n\nThe desert is famous for both its beauty and its dangers. Temperatures rise above fifty degrees by day and fall below zero at night, and strong winds can be extremely **destructive**, causing sandstorms that bury roads and vehicles. Old maps **describe** villages that have since disappeared completely, and local people **derive** many legends from their ruins.\n\nOn the fifth day the team began to **descend** into the canyon. Its **depth** surprised them: more than three hundred metres from the rim to the bottom. Lin wrote a careful **description** of the rock layers, which showed traces of ancient rivers and the **destruction** caused by earthquakes thousands of years ago.\n\n**Despite** their careful planning, things went wrong. A sandstorm damaged their radio, and for two days they could not contact the outside world. Some team members fell into **despair**, and one became so **desperate** that he wanted to turn back immediately. Dr. Ma remained calm. He reminded them that the canyon would not **destroy** them if they stayed together.\n\nWhen they finally returned, the team received praise from scientists around the world. Dr. Ma insists that Lin **deserves|deserve** most of the credit. A good explorer, he says, should never **despise** fear but should respect it. \"It is **desirable** to feel a **desire** for adventure,\" he says, \"but the **design** of every expedition must put safety first.\""},"46":{"w":["detect","detection","determination","determine","device","devil","devise","devote","dew","diagram","dial","dialect","diameter","diamond","diary","dictate","dictation","diet","differ","difference"],"title":"The Engineer's Diary","t":"At the age of seventy, my great-uncle still keeps a **diary** in which he records everything he observes in his garden: the amount of **dew** on the grass each morning, the **diameter** of the sunflowers, the first frost. He spent his career as an engineer, and he cannot stop measuring things.\n\nHis most famous invention was a small **device** that could **detect** tiny flaws in the industrial **diamonds|diamond** used in cutting tools. Before his work, the **detection** of such flaws required expensive machines; he managed to **devise** a cheaper method using a simple light and a lens. He drew the first **diagram** on the back of an envelope during a train journey.\n\nHis **determination** was legendary. When managers doubted his idea, he spent weekends in the workshop testing it, and he would **devote** every spare hour to improving the design. \"The **devil** is in the details,\" he likes to say, a phrase he picked up from a German colleague.\n\nHe grew up in a mountain village and still speaks a local **dialect** that even his children struggle to understand. As a boy, he practised writing through **dictation**: his teacher would **dictate** passages from old books, and the pupils copied them word for word. He believes this taught him patience.\n\nToday he keeps to a strict **diet** of mostly vegetables and fish, and he still uses an old telephone with a rotating **dial**. When I asked him what had made the biggest **difference** in his life, he said it was learning that people **differ** in how they think, and that no single method can **determine** success."},"47":{"w":["difficulty","digest","digital","diligent","dim","dimension","dip","direct","direction","directly","director","dirt","disable","disadvantage","disagree","disappear","disappoint","disapprove","disaster","discard"],"title":"The Director's First Film","t":"When the young film **director** Chen Lu began shooting her first feature film, everything seemed to go wrong. On the first day, heavy rain turned the location into a field of mud and **dirt**, and the crew could not film in the **dim** evening light. One actor began to **disagree** with every decision she made, and another seemed to **disappear** whenever he was needed.\n\nBy the end of the first week, the project looked like a **disaster**. The producers were worried; one of them said openly that he would **disapprove** any further spending, and Chen Lu feared she would **disappoint** everyone who had trusted her.\n\nBut she was **diligent**. She spent every night studying the footage on her **digital** camera, trying to **digest** what had gone wrong. She realised that her biggest **difficulty** was not the weather but her own lack of clear **direction**. She had been trying to please everyone, and the actors were confused about what she wanted.\n\nShe decided to **discard** half of her original script and focus on the central story. She spoke **directly** to each actor about their role and gave them more freedom. She also learned to turn the **disadvantage** of the rain into an advantage: several of the most powerful scenes in the finished film take place in storms, and the reflections on wet streets add a new **dimension** to the images.\n\nWhen the film was released, critics praised its honesty. Chen Lu admits that the early problems almost managed to **disable** the whole project. But whenever her confidence began to **dip**, she remembered her teacher's advice: \"A director's job is to **direct**, not to be loved.\""},"48":{"w":["discharge","discipline","disclose","discount","discourage","discover","discovery","discrimination","discussion","disease","disguise","disgust","dishonour","disk","dislike","dismiss","disorder","display","displease","disposal"],"title":"The Doctor's Discovery","t":"Dr. Lin had worked at the city hospital for fifteen years when she made an important **discovery**. While reviewing old patient records stored on a computer **disk**, she noticed that people from one particular neighbourhood were far more likely to suffer from a rare lung **disease**. Many had been treated and allowed to **discharge** themselves after a few days, only to return months later.\n\nAt first her colleagues tried to **discourage** her from investigating. Some feared it would **displease** the factory owners in the area; others simply did not want to **disclose** information that might cause panic. But Dr. Lin believed that the **discipline** of medicine demands honesty. She organised a **discussion** with local residents and showed them a **display** of maps and statistics.\n\nWhat she went on to **discover** was disturbing. A nearby chemical plant had been dumping waste near the river, and its owners had tried to **disguise** the problem by planting trees along the fence. When the evidence became public, residents reacted with **disgust**, and the scandal brought **dishonour** on the company.\n\nThe factory's managers first tried to **dismiss** the findings, blaming \"a minor seasonal **disorder**\". But the government ordered an investigation, and the company was forced to pay for the safe **disposal** of its waste.\n\nDr. Lin also spoke out against **discrimination**: some residents had been refused jobs elsewhere simply because they came from the area. Today the hospital offers a **discount** on health checks for local families. Dr. Lin admits that she has always had a strong **dislike** of conflict, but she says she could not stay silent."},"49":{"w":["dispose","dispute","dissatisfy","dissolve","distance","distant","distinct","distinction","distinguish","distress","distribute","distribution","district","disturb","ditch","dive","diverse","divide","division","divorce"],"title":"Two Brothers and a Field","t":"In a small farming **district** in the south, two brothers had been locked in a bitter **dispute** for years. Their father had left them a large piece of land but had never said how to **divide** it, and the **division** of the property had become a source of **distress** for the whole family.\n\nThe older brother wanted to keep the fields near the river; the younger argued that the short **distance** between the house and the river made those fields far more valuable. Neither was willing to compromise, and their relationship grew cold and **distant**. Their wives stopped speaking to each other, and one couple even talked of **divorce** because of the strain.\n\nFinally, an elderly village leader offered to help. She asked both brothers to explain what they wanted and listened patiently. She helped them **distinguish** between what they truly needed and what they simply wanted to win. \"There is a **distinct** difference,\" she said, \"between protecting your family and defeating your brother.\"\n\nShe proposed a creative solution: instead of splitting the land, they would farm it together and **distribute** the profits equally. She suggested they plant **diverse** crops, from rice to vegetables, to reduce the risk of a bad harvest, and that the **distribution** of work be agreed every spring. Any brother who wanted to **dispose** of his share would have to offer it to the other first.\n\nAt first the old quarrel continued to **disturb** their peace. The brothers argued over a drainage **ditch** and over whether to let their children **dive** into the river from the old bridge. But slowly the anger began to **dissolve**. Today their farm is one of the most successful in the area, the village leader has received a **distinction** from the county for her work, and neither brother, they say, has felt **dissatisfied|dissatisfy** since."},"50":{"w":["dock","document","domestic","dominate","donkey","dorm","dormitory","dose","dot","doubt","doubtful","doubtless","downstairs","downward","dozen","draft","drag","dragon","drain","drama"],"title":"Dormitory Drama","t":"Every university **dormitory** has its own stories, but ours seemed to produce more **drama** than most. In my first year, I shared a small room on the third floor with three other students, and our **dorm** quickly became famous throughout the building.\n\nThe trouble began in September, when the girls in the room below complained that we were too noisy. Every time we dropped a book or moved a chair, they heard it. One evening a girl from **downstairs** knocked on our door holding a written **document** that listed every noise we had made that week.\n\nMy roommate Jiang, a drama student with a talent for performance, decided to apologise in style. She bought a **dozen** cakes, painted a red **dragon** on a card and wrote an elaborate **draft** of an apology letter. **Doubtless**, she thought, this would solve the problem. But when she presented it, the neighbours were **doubtful**; they suspected it was a joke.\n\nThings got worse when the pipes in our bathroom began to leak. Water would **drain** away only slowly, and a small **dot** of damp on the floor grew larger each day. Eventually the water seeped **downward** into the room below, and our neighbours were understandably furious.\n\nIn the end the university sent a plumber, who cleared the pipes with a single **dose** of chemical cleaner. Jiang and I helped **drag** the neighbours' wet carpet outside to dry, and working together, we got to know them. One came from a farming family and kept a photograph of a **donkey** on her desk; another was studying **domestic** policy and dreamed of working near the **dock** of a big port city. By the end of the year we were all close friends, and nobody tried to **dominate** the others. If you had told me in September that this would happen, I would have had no **doubt** you were joking."},"51":{"w":["dramatic","drawer","drawing","dread","dream","drift","drill","drip","drop","drought","drown","drug","drum","drunk","duck","due","dull","dumb","dump","durable"],"title":"The Summer of the Drought","t":"The summer I turned twelve was the driest anyone in our village could remember. The **drought** lasted four months, and the river that usually ran beside our house shrank to a thin, **dull** line of mud. My grandfather, who had farmed the land for fifty years, said he had never seen such a **dramatic** change.\n\nEvery morning I listened for the **drip** of water from the old tap in the yard, but it was almost always silent. My father decided to **drill** a new well, and the whole village came to watch. When water finally gushed out, people cheered and someone began to beat a **drum** in celebration. Even the **ducks|duck** in the dry pond seemed to understand.\n\nMy grandfather kept a box of old maps in a **drawer** of his desk. One evening he showed me a **drawing** he had made as a young man of the same river in flood, so wide and fast that a child could **drown** in it. \"Nature has a long memory,\" he said. \"We have a short one.\"\n\nThat summer taught me to **dread** waste. We learned to save every **drop** of water, to reuse washing water on the garden and to **dump** nothing into the riverbed. A doctor visited the village with medicine, and he warned that dirty water could be as dangerous as any **drug**.\n\nNot everyone coped well. One neighbour came home **drunk** every night, and another simply stopped talking, as if the heat had struck him **dumb**. But most people helped each other. When the rains finally came, clouds would **drift** over the hills for days before the first heavy storm, which everyone agreed was long **due**. I still keep a **durable** old bucket from that year in my garage, and I still **dream** about the sound of that first rain."},"52":{"w":["duration","dusk","dust","duty","dwelling","dye","dying","dynamic","eager","eagle","earn","earnest","earthquake","ease","easily","eastern","echo","economic","economical","economics"],"title":"After the Earthquake","t":"When a powerful **earthquake** struck a mountain region in the **eastern** part of the province, thousands of people lost their homes. Many families had lived in the same **dwelling** for generations, and in a few terrible seconds those houses were reduced to **dust**.\n\nAmong the first volunteers to arrive was a young economist named Lu Fang. She had studied **economics** at university and had always been interested in how communities recover after disasters. But she soon realised that **economic** theories meant little when people needed food and shelter. \"My **duty**,\" she said, \"was simply to help.\"\n\nShe worked from dawn until **dusk**, handing out blankets and organising temporary schools. She was **eager** to listen to people's stories and **earnest** in her efforts to understand their needs, and many villagers said that her calm presence helped to **ease** their fears. She also noticed an old man nursing a wounded **eagle** he had found on the mountainside; he said that caring for it gave him something to live for.\n\nThe **duration** of the crisis was longer than anyone had expected. Aftershocks continued for weeks, and at night the **echo** of falling rocks could be heard across the valley. Some injured people lay **dying** in remote villages that rescue teams could not **easily** reach.\n\nWhen the emergency phase ended, Lu Fang helped residents plan a more **economical** way to rebuild, using local materials and simple designs. She encouraged women to set up small businesses selling cloth coloured with natural **dye**. \"Recovery must be **dynamic**,\" she said. \"People need to **earn** a living, not just receive help.\""},"53":{"w":["economy","edge","edition","editor","editorial","educate","education","effect","effective","efficiency","efficient","effort","eighteen","eighty","elaborate","elastic","elbow","elder","elderly","elect"],"title":"The Editor at Eighty","t":"At **eighty**, Mrs. Qian is still the chief **editor** of a small regional newspaper. She began working there at **eighteen** as a typist, and over six decades she has seen the paper survive every kind of crisis, from political change to the collapse of the local **economy**.\n\nShe is known for her **elaborate** attention to detail. Every evening she reads each page of the next day's **edition** with a red pen, correcting grammar and checking facts. Her **editorial** column, published every Friday, discusses issues such as **education**, transport and the needs of **elderly** residents, and readers say her writing is clear, fair and surprisingly modern.\n\nYounger journalists admire her **efficiency**. She can write a complete article in twenty minutes and runs the newsroom in a remarkably **efficient** way. But she insists that speed matters less than **effort**. \"Good journalism must **educate** people,\" she says, \"not just entertain them.\"\n\nMrs. Qian believes the paper has had a real **effect** on the town. When the city council planned to close a primary school, her articles helped residents organise an **effective** campaign to keep it open. Two years later she supported a young candidate in the local council race; when he was **elected|elect**, he thanked her publicly.\n\nShe is small and thin, with sharp eyes and a habit of resting her **elbow** on the desk while she reads, and her colleagues treat her as the respected **elder** of the newsroom. Asked why she has not retired, she smiles and says that a newspaper needs an **elastic** mind more than young legs. \"Journalism keeps me on the **edge**,\" she says. \"That's why I am still here.\""},"54":{"w":["election","electric","electrical","electricity","electron","electronic","electronics","element","elementary","elephant","elevator","eliminate","elimination","elsewhere","embarrass","embrace","emerge","emergency","emit","emotion"],"title":"A Day at the Electronics Fair","t":"Every autumn our city hosts a large **electronics** fair, and this year I went with my younger brother, who is in his last year of **elementary** school. He is obsessed with anything **electronic**, from robots to game consoles, and he could not wait to see the latest inventions.\n\nThe fair took place in a huge hall that seemed to hum with **electricity**. We took a glass **elevator** to the top floor, where companies displayed **electric** cars, **electrical** tools and solar panels. One engineer explained that every device depends on the movement of the **electron**, and that understanding this basic **element** of physics is the key to all modern technology.\n\nMy brother's favourite stand belonged to a start-up that builds robots for zoos. Its latest model, a giant mechanical **elephant**, could walk, raise its trunk and **emit** a loud trumpeting sound. Visitors gathered around it with obvious **emotion**, and my brother laughed so loudly that I felt a little **embarrassed|embarrass**.\n\nIn another area, a company demonstrated a system designed to **eliminate** waste in factories. The **elimination** of unnecessary energy use, the speaker said, could cut costs by half. She also showed how her system responds to an **emergency**, such as a power cut, by switching automatically to batteries.\n\nAs we were leaving, a small drone seemed to **emerge** from behind a curtain and flew over our heads, to the delight of the crowd. My brother wanted to stay, but the hall was closing. \"We can find more robots **elsewhere**,\" I promised. He has already decided on his promise for the next student council **election**: a robotics club for every class. He wants every school to **embrace** technology the way he does."},"55":{"w":["emotional","emperor","emphasis","emphasize","empire","employ","employee","employer","employment","enable","enclose","encounter","encourage","ending","endless","endure","enemy","energy","enforce","engage"],"title":"Restoring the Past","t":"When Zhou Jie applied for a job at the Palace Museum, he did not expect to be hired. Thousands of people wanted to work there, and the museum could **employ** only a few each year. But his passion for the history of the Qing **empire** impressed the interview panel, and he became the youngest **employee** in the restoration department.\n\nHis **employer** placed great **emphasis** on patience. On his first day his supervisor told him that every object in the museum had belonged to someone, perhaps even an **emperor**, and that restoring it was a form of respect. She liked to **emphasize** that mistakes could not be undone.\n\nThe work was slow and sometimes frustrating. Restoring a single painted screen could take two years, and Zhou often felt that the task was **endless**. He had to **endure** long hours in silent rooms, bent over tiny cracks in the wood. Yet he found it deeply **emotional** to watch faded colours return to life under his brush.\n\nHe also learned that the past has a powerful **enemy**: time, dust and human carelessness. The museum must **enforce** strict rules to protect its collection, and visitors who **encounter** a guard may be reminded not to touch anything. Zhou believes that these rules **enable** future generations to enjoy the treasures.\n\nHis job has also given him new **energy** for teaching. He now runs workshops that **engage** children in simple restoration projects, and he likes to **encourage** them to ask questions. At the end of each session he gives every child a small card **enclosed|enclose** in a paper envelope, with a picture of the object they helped repair. Every story, he tells them, deserves a good **ending**, and he hopes some of them will one day find **employment** in heritage work themselves."},"56":{"w":["engine","engineer","engineering","enhance","enlarge","enormous","enroll","ensure","enter","entertain","entertainment","enthusiasm","enthusiastic","entire","entitle","entrance","entry","envelope","environment","envy"],"title":"The Engineering Camp","t":"When my niece decided to **enroll** in a summer **engineering** camp, the whole family was surprised. She had always been more interested in music and **entertainment** than in machines. But after watching a documentary about a young **engineer** who designed solar-powered cars, she filled in the application form, sealed it in an **envelope** and posted it the same day.\n\nThe camp was held in an **enormous** university workshop. On the first morning the students walked through the main **entrance** and found rows of tools, computers and an old car **engine** waiting to be taken apart. Their task for the week was to build a small vehicle that would **ensure** the safe delivery of an egg across the room.\n\nMy niece's team showed remarkable **enthusiasm**. They worked through lunch, argued about designs and tested every idea. One teammate suggested they **enlarge** the wheels to make the car more stable; another wanted to **enhance** its speed with a stronger motor. My niece, unexpectedly **enthusiastic**, kept notes in a notebook she later called \"my **entire** summer in one book\".\n\nOn the final day, every team had to **enter** its vehicle in a competition. Families came to watch, and the teachers tried to **entertain** them with jokes while the students made last-minute repairs. My niece's car carried its egg safely across the finish line, and her team's **entry** won second prize, which **entitled|entitle** them to a visit to a car factory.\n\nThe teachers also talked about the **environment**, explaining how engineers can design greener technology. My niece says she now feels a little **envy** whenever she sees engineers on television. \"Next year,\" she says, \"I want first prize.\""},"57":{"w":["episode","equal","equality","equation","equip","equipment","equivalent","era","erect","error","escape","especially","essay","essential","establish","establishment","estate","estimate","evacuate","evaluate"],"title":"The Night of the Typhoon","t":"When a powerful typhoon approached the coast last autumn, the local government decided to **evacuate** a small fishing village on an island. The order was not popular. Many residents were elderly, and some had lived through every storm of the past **era** without ever leaving home. \"We have survived worse,\" one old fisherman said.\n\nThe rescue team had to **estimate** how many people remained and **evaluate** the condition of the roads. Their leader, a calm woman named Captain Hou, explained that the most **essential** task was to make sure no one was left behind. Her team was well **equipped|equip**, with boats, radios and medical **equipment**, but she knew that technology could not replace trust.\n\nShe went from house to house, listening to each family. She understood that for many villagers their small **estate** of land and boats was their whole life, and that leaving felt like a betrayal. She promised that their property would be protected and that every person would be treated as an **equal**, with complete **equality** in the shelters.\n\nOne **episode** from that night became famous. An elderly woman refused to leave until she had finished writing a short **essay** for her grandson about the history of the village, and a man tried to **escape** back to his house to rescue his dog. Captain Hou did not lose patience. \"Every **error** in a crisis can cost a life,\" she said, \"so we take our time with each person, **especially** the old.\"\n\nThe typhoon destroyed many houses, but nobody died. Afterwards, the government helped the villagers **establish** a new shelter on higher ground and **erect** stronger sea walls, and the **establishment** of an early-warning system has since become a model for other islands. When reporters asked Captain Hou how she had done it, she smiled. \"There is no simple **equation**,\" she said. \"But trust is worth the **equivalent** of a hundred boats.\""},"58":{"w":["evaporate","eve","event","eventually","everyday","evidence","evident","evil","evolution","evolve","exact","exactly","exaggerate","examination","examine","exceed","exceedingly","excellent","exception","excess"],"title":"The Evolution Exam","t":"Every student in our biology class remembers the final **examination** on **evolution**. Professor Li, an **excellent** teacher with a sharp sense of humour, had warned us that he would **examine** not only our knowledge but also our ability to reason from **evidence**.\n\nOn the **eve** of the exam, our study group met in the library. We tried to memorise **exactly** how species **evolve** over millions of years, but it soon became **evident** that memorising was not enough. One friend kept trying to **exaggerate** the difficulty of the topic, calling it \"**exceedingly** impossible\", which made everyone laugh.\n\nThe exam itself was an unusual **event**. Instead of asking us to repeat definitions, Professor Li gave us a series of real cases: a lake whose water was beginning to **evaporate** faster each year, birds whose beaks were changing shape, insects that had become resistant to chemicals. We had to explain the changes and predict what might **eventually** happen.\n\nThe questions demanded **exact** reasoning. Answers that piled up **excess** detail without logic received low marks; the professor did not want us to **exceed** the word limit just to show off. He also included one trick question about whether evolution is \"good\" or \"**evil**\". The correct answer, of course, was that evolution has no moral direction.\n\nAlmost everyone passed, with only one **exception**: a student who had skipped classes all term. Years later, I still think about that exam whenever I notice small changes in **everyday** life, such as the sparrows in my garden. Professor Li taught us that science is not a list of facts but a way of looking at the world."},"59":{"w":["excessive","exchange","excite","excitement","exciting","exclaim","exclude","exclusive","exclusively","excursion","execute","executive","exert","exhaust","exhaustion","exhibit","exhibition","exist","existence","exit"],"title":"A Company Excursion","t":"Last month our company organised an **excursion** to a new science museum for all its staff. The idea came from our chief **executive**, who believes that employees work better when they **exchange** ideas outside the office. Not everyone was convinced; some colleagues complained that a museum trip was hardly **exciting**.\n\nThe museum's main **exhibition** was about energy. One room displayed a giant model of a power station; another showed how cars **exhaust** fumes into the air. A guide explained that **excessive** use of fossil fuels is one of the main causes of climate change, and that the **existence** of many species is now at risk.\n\nThe most popular **exhibit** was an interactive area where visitors could **exert** force on a bicycle to generate electricity. My colleagues competed to see who could light the most bulbs. Within ten minutes several of them were **exhausted|exhaust**, and one fell off the bike in **exhaustion**, to great **excitement** from the crowd. \"I didn't know we could still **excite** each other like this,\" our manager **exclaimed|exclaim**, laughing.\n\nThe museum also offered an **exclusive** tour of its research laboratories, open **exclusively** to groups who had booked in advance. Scientists showed us how they **execute** complex experiments and invited questions, taking care not to **exclude** those of us who knew nothing about physics.\n\nAt the **exit**, each visitor was given a small card with a question: \"What will you change tomorrow?\" Many of us still keep it on our desks. The trip reminded me that new ideas can **exist** anywhere, if we are willing to step outside our usual routine."},"60":{"w":["expand","expansion","expectation","expel","expend","expenditure","expense","experience","experiment","experimental","expert","explain","explanation","explode","exploit","explore","explosion","explosive","export","expose"],"title":"The Arctic Expedition","t":"For many years Dr. Zhou has been an **expert** in polar science. Last summer she led a team to **explore** a remote part of the Arctic, where melting ice has begun to **expose** rocks that have not seen sunlight for thousands of years.\n\nThe trip was expensive. The total **expenditure** came to more than two million yuan, and Dr. Zhou had to **explain** every **expense** to the university's finance committee. Some members questioned whether it was wise to **expend** so much money on a single trip. Her **explanation** was simple: the Arctic is changing faster than any other region, and understanding it is essential.\n\nHer team carried out an **experimental** study of how the ice reacts to rising temperatures. In one **experiment**, they drilled deep into a glacier to measure air bubbles trapped inside. The work was dangerous. Once, a gas pocket beneath the ice caused a small **explosion**, sending fragments of ice into the air. Nobody was hurt, but the team learned to treat the glacier as almost **explosive**.\n\nDr. Zhou also warned against efforts to **exploit** the region. As the ice melts, companies hope to **expand** oil drilling and **export** minerals, and the rapid **expansion** of shipping routes could cause serious pollution. \"We should not allow the Arctic to **explode** into a new industrial zone,\" she said.\n\nThe team returned with more data than anyone had expected, far beyond their own **expectations|expectation**, and Dr. Zhou describes the trip as the most intense **experience** of her life. It was not without difficulties: early in the journey, she admits, the team was forced to **expel** a member who repeatedly ignored safety rules. \"Science needs passion,\" she said, \"but it also needs discipline.\""},"61":{"w":["exposure","express","expression","extend","extension","extensive","extent","exterior","external","extinct","extra","extraordinary","extreme","extremely","eyesight","fabric","facility","fact","factor","faculty"],"title":"The New Science Building","t":"When the university decided to **extend** its science **faculty**, it planned a new building with **extensive** laboratories and a large greenhouse. The project attracted **extraordinary** attention, partly because of its unusual design: its **exterior** was covered in a light **fabric** made from recycled plastic that changes colour in the sunlight.\n\nThe architect explained that the shape of the building was not just decoration. One key **factor** in the design was protection from **extreme** weather. The fabric reduces **exposure** to strong sunlight in summer and keeps heat inside in winter. \"In **fact**,\" she said, \"the building uses forty per cent less energy than an ordinary one.\"\n\nThe new **facility** also includes an **extension** for the biology department, where researchers study species that are close to becoming **extinct**. They use **external** cameras to observe birds nesting on the roof, and they have discovered that some rare species are **extremely** sensitive to noise.\n\nNot everyone was pleased. Some students complained that the project had taken **extra** money from other departments. To what **extent**, they asked, should a university spend money on buildings rather than people? The president responded in a public letter, trying to **express** the university's long-term vision.\n\nAt the opening ceremony, a retired professor who had taught there for forty years gave a moving speech. His **eyesight** had weakened and he could barely read his notes, but his **expression** of joy was clear to everyone. \"Science,\" he said, \"is the art of looking carefully, with or without good eyes.\""},"62":{"w":["fade","fahrenheit","failure","faint","fair","fairly","faith","faithful","false","fame","familiar","famine","fan","fancy","fantasy","fare","farewell","farmer","farther","fashion"],"title":"The Singing Farmer","t":"Old Mr. Tang was an ordinary **farmer** until a short video of him singing in his fields went viral. Overnight he gained national **fame**. Millions of people watched him plough the land while singing folk songs in a strong, slightly rough voice, and his **fan** club soon grew to more than a hundred thousand members.\n\nThe story sounded like a modern **fantasy**, but Mr. Tang's life had been far from easy. As a child he had survived a terrible **famine**, when his family had little to eat for months. He remembered his mother's **faint** voice singing to him at night, and he said that her songs gave him **faith** that things would get better.\n\nFame did not change him much. He refused **fancy** clothes and expensive dinners and continued to work the land every day. When a television company invited him to perform in the capital, he insisted on paying his own train **fare**. It was only **fair**, he said, to treat everyone the same, rich or poor.\n\nHe had little patience for **false** rumours, either. When a newspaper claimed that his videos were staged, he replied calmly, \"I'm **fairly** sure I know how to sing in my own field.\" The story soon began to **fade**, and his **faithful** audience kept growing.\n\nMr. Tang said his biggest **failure** was never learning to read music; singing simply felt more **familiar** than reading notes. He disliked travelling any **farther** than the nearest town and once complained that the capital was \"a hundred degrees **Fahrenheit** and full of noise\". Yet his songs have since appeared in films and even **fashion** shows. At his last public concert, he waved **farewell** to the crowd and said simply, \"Go home and sing.\""},"63":{"w":["fashionable","fasten","fatal","fate","father-in-law","fatigue","fault","faulty","favor","favorable","favorite","fear","fearful","feasible","feast","feather","feature","federal","fee","feeble"],"title":"The Spring Festival Feast","t":"Every Spring Festival, my **father-in-law** prepares an enormous **feast** for the whole family. It is his **favorite** day of the year, and he spends a week planning the menu in a notebook marked with a single peacock **feather**.\n\nThis year, however, things went wrong. He was suffering from **fatigue** after a long illness and looked unusually **feeble**. My wife suggested ordering food from a restaurant, but he refused. \"It is my **fate** to cook for this family,\" he joked, \"and a small cold will not change it.\"\n\nThe first problem was the oven, which turned out to be **faulty**; the bread burned within minutes. My father-in-law insisted that it was not his **fault**. Then the electricity went off, and we had to **fasten** a torch to the kitchen wall so that he could see. My wife looked **fearful**, but he laughed and said he had cooked in far worse conditions during his years in the army.\n\nWe asked him to do us a **favor** and rest, but he would not. Instead, he gave each of us a job. The children washed vegetables, I chopped meat, and my wife set the table in a **fashionable** style she had seen in a magazine. By evening the meal was ready, and the mood could not have been more **favorable** for a celebration.\n\nThe main **feature** of the dinner was a whole fish cooked in the style of his hometown. During the meal, a cousin who works in a **federal** office in Canada called by video and joked about the **fee** he had paid to change his flight home. My father-in-law raised his glass and said that the only **fear** worth having is the fear of not trying. We agreed that his plan to cook alone had been barely **feasible**, but none of the evening's mistakes had proved **fatal**."},"64":{"w":["feed","feedback","feeling","fellow","female","fence","fertile","fertilizer","festival","fetch","fever","fiction","fierce","fight","figure","file","filter","final","finally","finance"],"title":"The Fertilizer Experiment","t":"Last spring, a group of agricultural students at our university decided to test whether natural **fertilizer** could replace chemicals on small farms. Their project was supported by a local **finance** company and a professor who had spent his career studying **fertile** soils in the south.\n\nThe students worked with a **female** farmer named Mrs. Huang, who had been growing vegetables on the same land for thirty years. They divided her field into two parts with a wooden **fence**. On one side they used chemical fertilizer; on the other, a mixture of compost and animal manure. Each week they would **fetch** soil samples and **file** reports in the university database.\n\nThe work was hard. One student fell ill with a high **fever** after a week of rain, and others struggled with the smell. They had to **filter** water samples to test for pollution, and **fierce** storms damaged several rows of plants. But they refused to give up. \"This is a **fight** for the future of farming,\" one student said.\n\nMrs. Huang gave them honest **feedback** throughout the season. She showed them how to **feed** the soil with crop waste and how to judge the health of plants from the colour of their leaves, and she told them about **fellow** farmers who had lost money after years of using too many chemicals.\n\nThe **final** results were clear. The natural side produced slightly fewer vegetables, but their quality was higher and the soil was healthier. When the students presented their **figures|figure** at an agricultural **festival**, the audience was impressed. **Finally**, Mrs. Huang said, someone had proved with data what farmers had always sensed. \"It's not **fiction**,\" she said. \"It's my **feeling** after thirty years.\""},"65":{"w":["financial","finding","finger","fireman","firm","fisherman","fist","fix","flag","flame","flare","flash","flat","flee","fleet","flesh","flexible","float","flock","flood"],"title":"The Night of the Flood","t":"Last July, heavy rain caused a sudden **flood** in a fishing village on the southern coast. The water rose so quickly that many families had to **flee** their homes in the middle of the night, carrying only what they could hold in their hands.\n\nAmong the first to help was a **fisherman** named Chen, who used his boat to rescue neighbours from rooftops. He was soon joined by a **fleet** of small boats from nearby villages and a team of firefighters. One **fireman** later described how Chen worked for twelve hours without stopping, one **fist** tight on the wheel and his **fingers|finger** bleeding from the ropes.\n\nAt one point a gas pipe broke, and a **flame** shot out of the water near a flooded shop. The firefighters fired a **flare** to warn other boats to keep away, and a **flash** of red light lit up the dark sky. Chen steered his boat around the danger and continued his rescue work.\n\nBy morning, the land around the village lay **flat** and covered in mud. Broken furniture would **float** past the remaining houses, and a **flock** of birds circled overhead, searching for food. Rescuers raised a red **flag** on the school roof to show people where they could find shelter.\n\nThe government later published a report on the **financial** cost of the disaster. Its main **finding** was that the village needed better drainage and a more **flexible** emergency plan, and a local engineering **firm** offered to **fix** the sea wall for free. Chen received a medal for bravery, but he refused to talk about it. \"We are all **flesh** and blood,\" he said. \"We help each other.\""}}});
